var documenterSearchIndex = {"docs":
[{"category":"section","location":"references/#References","page":"References","text":"D. Holmes and G. Muratore. Computations in equivariant Gromov-Witten theory of GKM spaces (2025), submitted, arXiv:2509.07562 [math.AG].\n\n\n\nD. Holmes and G. Muratore. GKMtools.jl, A Julia package for GKM spaces and their equivariant Gromov-Witten theory (2025).\n\n\n\nD. Holmes and Y. Schuler. Experiments with membranes, maps and sheaves (2026), in preparation.\n\n\n\nH. Liu, boxcounting, A collection of SageMath code for box-counting computations (2025).\n\n\n\n","title":"References"},{"category":"section","location":"cy5_overview/#GW_CY5.jl","page":"The package GW_CY5.jl","text":"A Julia package to compute the membrane indices Omega_beta of toric CY5s.\n\nWe build on the Julia package GKMtools.jl [HM25a, HM25b] to compute equivariant Gromov-Witten invariants of the relevant spaces.","title":"GW_CY5.jl"},{"category":"section","location":"cy5_overview/#Contents","page":"The package GW_CY5.jl","text":"Installation instructions\nPipeline: explanation of the workflow of analyzing any given example with this package.\nSpaces: list of spaces that are implemented to work with the pipeline.\nExamples:\nJulia code in the paper: a mirror of all Julia code contained in our paper.\nDetails of the Pipeline: an example of the Pipeline, worked out in detail, showing some internal details.\nEvidence: for each numerical experiment cited as Evidence in the paper, we provide code to reproduce the stated results.","title":"Contents"},{"category":"section","location":"cy5_overview/#gw_installation","page":"The package GW_CY5.jl","text":"First, install OSCAR using the instructions here. After that, open Julia and install GW_CY5.jl by typing the following code. This will automatically install the package GKMtools.jl on which GW_CY5.jl builds.\n\njulia> using Pkg;\njulia> Pkg.add(url=\"https://github.com/dh604/CY5\")\n\nYou can now load the package by typing the following line.\n\njulia> using Oscar, GKMtools, GW_CY5","title":"Installation"},{"category":"section","location":"spaces/#Spaces","page":"Spaces","text":"On this page, we list the implemented spaces and constructor functions that are compatible with the Pipeline. This means that they can be used in conjunction with get_Omega_prediction and hence with get_Omega_beta and its optional argument check_predictions=true.\n\nnote: Note\nOnly those functions that return a 5-dimensional result are compatible with the Pipeline. Functions returning a lower-dimensional result are listed because they are ingredients for 5-dimensional constructions.","title":"Spaces"},{"category":"section","location":"spaces/#Strip-geometries","page":"Spaces","text":"","title":"Strip geometries"},{"category":"section","location":"spaces/#spaces_closed_vertex","page":"Spaces","text":"","title":"Closed vertex"},{"category":"section","location":"spaces/#GW-Gauge-correspondence","page":"Spaces","text":"","title":"GW-Gauge correspondence"},{"category":"section","location":"spaces/#From-3-folds","page":"Spaces","text":"","title":"From 3-folds"},{"category":"section","location":"spaces/#From-4-folds","page":"Spaces","text":"","title":"From 4-folds"},{"category":"section","location":"spaces/#Further-examples","page":"Spaces","text":"","title":"Further examples"},{"category":"function","location":"spaces/#GW_CY5.Ar_times_C1","page":"Spaces","text":"Ar_times_C1(r::Int64) -> GKMtools.AbstractGKM_graph\n\nReturn mathcalA_rtimesmathbbC^1, where r is the number of rational curves in the chain.\n\nExample\n\njulia> G = Ar_times_C1(2)\nGKM graph with 3 nodes, valency 3 and axial function:\n2 -> 1 => (1, -1, 0)\n3 -> 2 => (1, -2, 0)\nStandalone flags:\n1.2 => (0, 0, 1)\n1.3 => (1, 0, 0)\n2.3 => (0, 0, 1)\n3.2 => (0, 0, 1)\n3.3 => (-1, 3, 0)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GW_CY5.Ar_times_C1"},{"category":"function","location":"spaces/#GW_CY5.Ar_times_C3","page":"Spaces","text":"Ar_times_C3(r::Int64) -> GKMtools.AbstractGKM_graph\n\nReturn mathcalA_rtimesmathbbC^3, where r is the number of rational curves in the chain. The space is equivariantly Calabi-Yau.\n\nExample\n\njulia> G = Ar_times_C3(2)\nGKM graph with 3 nodes, valency 5 and axial function:\n2,1 -> 1,1 => (1, -1, 0, 0, 0)\n3,1 -> 2,1 => (1, -2, 0, 0, 0)\nStandalone flags:\n1,1.2 => (0, -1, 0, -1, -1)\n1,1.3 => (1, 0, 0, 0, 0)\n1,1.4 => (0, 0, 0, 1, 0)\n1,1.5 => (0, 0, 0, 0, 1)\n2,1.3 => (0, -1, 0, -1, -1)\n2,1.4 => (0, 0, 0, 1, 0)\n2,1.5 => (0, 0, 0, 0, 1)\n3,1.2 => (0, -1, 0, -1, -1)\n3,1.3 => (-1, 3, 0, 0, 0)\n3,1.4 => (0, 0, 0, 1, 0)\n3,1.5 => (0, 0, 0, 0, 1)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GW_CY5.Ar_times_C3"},{"category":"function","location":"spaces/#GW_CY5.gkm_3d_strip","page":"Spaces","text":"gkm_3d_strip(ups_and_downs::Vector{Int64}; equiCY::Bool=false) -> GKMtools.AbstractGKM_graph\n\nCreate the GKM graph of the 3d strip geometry described by ups_and_downs (see [HS26, Section 2.1]).\n\nArguments\n\nups_and_downs: a vector of non-zero integers with the following meaning in terms of [HS26, (18)]: there will   be ups_and_downs[1] flags up (if positive) or down (if negative), followed by ups_and_downs[2] flags up or down,   and so on.\nequiCY: If this optional argument with default value false is set to true, the returned space will be linearized to be equivariantly Calabi-Yau.\n\nWeight convention:\n\nIn terms of [HS26, (18)]: flags pointing up have weight epsilon_2, flags pointing down have weight epsilon_3, and the flag at vertex 1 pointing left has weight epsilon_1.\n\nExample\n\nLet us see the same example once with equiCY=false (default) and once with equiCY=true.\n\njulia> G = gkm_3d_strip([1, -1, 1]) # equiCY=false\nGKM graph with 3 nodes, valency 3 and axial function:\n2 -> 1 => (1, 0, -1)\n3 -> 2 => (1, -1, -1)\nStandalone flags:\n1.2 => (0, 1, 0)\n1.3 => (1, 0, 0)\n2.3 => (0, 0, 1)\n3.2 => (-1, 1, 2)\n3.3 => (0, 1, 0)\n\njulia> G = gkm_3d_strip([1, -1, 1]; equiCY=true)\nGKM graph with 3 nodes, valency 3 and axial function:\n2 -> 1 => (-1, 1)\n3 -> 2 => (-1, 0)\nStandalone flags:\n1.2 => (0, 1)\n1.3 => (-1, 0)\n2.3 => (0, -1)\n3.2 => (1, -1)\n3.3 => (0, 1)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GW_CY5.gkm_3d_strip"},{"category":"function","location":"spaces/#GW_CY5.gkm_5d_strip","page":"Spaces","text":"gkm_5d_strip(ups_and_downs::Vector{Int64}; equiCY::Bool=false) -> GKMtools.AbstractGKM_graph\n\nReturn the product of the output of gkm_3d_strip with mathbbC^2, where the latter has weights epsilon_4 epsilon_5.\n\nArguments\n\nups_and_downs: Same meaning as in gkm_3d_strip\nequiCY: If this optional argument with default value false is set to true, the result is linearized   to be equivariantly Calabi-Yau. Note that the underlying 3d strip geometry is not taken to be equivariantly   Calabi-Yau.\n\nExample\n\nLet us produce the example of gkm_3d_strip times mathbbC^2.\n\njulia> G = gkm_5d_strip([1, -1, 1])\nGKM graph with 3 nodes, valency 5 and axial function:\n2,1 -> 1,1 => (1, 0, -1, 0, 0)\n3,1 -> 2,1 => (1, -1, -1, 0, 0)\nStandalone flags:\n1,1.2 => (0, 1, 0, 0, 0)\n1,1.3 => (1, 0, 0, 0, 0)\n1,1.4 => (0, 0, 0, 1, 0)\n1,1.5 => (0, 0, 0, 0, 1)\n2,1.3 => (0, 0, 1, 0, 0)\n2,1.4 => (0, 0, 0, 1, 0)\n2,1.5 => (0, 0, 0, 0, 1)\n3,1.2 => (-1, 1, 2, 0, 0)\n3,1.3 => (0, 1, 0, 0, 0)\n3,1.4 => (0, 0, 0, 1, 0)\n3,1.5 => (0, 0, 0, 0, 1)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GW_CY5.gkm_5d_strip"},{"category":"function","location":"spaces/#GW_CY5.minus_one_minus_one_chain_3d","page":"Spaces","text":"minus_one_minus_one_chain_3d(r::Int64) -> GKMtools.AbstractGKM_graph\n\nReturn a chain of mathcalO(-1)oplusmathcalO(-1) bundles of length r. The result is Calabi-Yau but not equivariantly Calabi–Yau and coincides with the output of gkm_3d_strip with ups_and_downs = [-1, 1, -1, ...].\n\nExample\n\nLet us produce the chain of length 2.\n\njulia> GW_CY5.minus_one_minus_one_chain_3d(2)\nGKM graph with 3 nodes, valency 3 and axial function:\n2 -> 1 => (1, -1, 0)\n3 -> 2 => (1, -1, -1)\nStandalone flags:\n1.2 => (0, 0, 1)\n1.3 => (1, 0, 0)\n2.3 => (0, 1, 0)\n3.2 => (-1, 2, 1)\n3.3 => (0, 0, 1)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GW_CY5.minus_one_minus_one_chain_3d"},{"category":"function","location":"spaces/#GW_CY5.minus_one_minus_one_chain","page":"Spaces","text":"minus_one_minus_one_chain(r::Int64) -> GKMtools.AbstractGKM_graph\n\nReturn a chain of mathcalO(-1)oplusmathcalO(-1) bundles of length r, product with mathbbC^2. The result is equivariantly Calabi-Yau and coincides with the output of gkm_5d_strip with ups_and_downs = [-1, 1, -1, ...].\n\nExample\n\nLet us produce the chain of length 2.\n\njulia> G = minus_one_minus_one_chain(2)\nGKM graph with 3 nodes, valency 5 and axial function:\n2,1 -> 1,1 => (1, -1, 0, 0, 0)\n3,1 -> 2,1 => (1, 0, 0, 1, 1)\nStandalone flags:\n1,1.2 => (0, -1, 0, -1, -1)\n1,1.3 => (1, 0, 0, 0, 0)\n1,1.4 => (0, 0, 0, 1, 0)\n1,1.5 => (0, 0, 0, 0, 1)\n2,1.3 => (0, 1, 0, 0, 0)\n2,1.4 => (0, 0, 0, 1, 0)\n2,1.5 => (0, 0, 0, 0, 1)\n3,1.2 => (-1, 1, 0, -1, -1)\n3,1.3 => (0, -1, 0, -1, -1)\n3,1.4 => (0, 0, 0, 1, 0)\n3,1.5 => (0, 0, 0, 0, 1)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GW_CY5.minus_one_minus_one_chain"},{"category":"function","location":"spaces/#GW_CY5.gkm_3d_closed_vertex","page":"Spaces","text":"gkm_3d_closed_vertex() -> GKMtools.AbstractGKM_graph\n\nReturn the GKM graph of the 3d closed vertex geometry. The weights are defined to agree with [HS26, Figure 4].\n\nExample\n\njulia> gkm_3d_closed_vertex()\nGKM graph with 4 nodes, valency 3 and axial function:\n0 -> 1 => (-1//2, 1//2, 1//2)\n2 -> 0 => (-1//2, 1//2, -1//2)\n3 -> 0 => (-1//2, -1//2, 1//2)\nStandalone flags:\n1.2 => (0, 1, 0)\n1.3 => (0, 0, 1)\n2.2 => (1, 0, 0)\n2.3 => (0, 0, 1)\n3.2 => (1, 0, 0)\n3.3 => (0, 1, 0)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GW_CY5.gkm_3d_closed_vertex"},{"category":"function","location":"spaces/#GW_CY5.gkm_5d_closed_vertex","page":"Spaces","text":"gkm_5d_closed_vertex(; equiCY::Bool=false) -> GKMtools.AbstractGKM_graph\n\nReturn the product of the output of gkm_3d_closed_vertex with mathbbC^2, where the latter has weights epsilon_4 epsilon_5.\n\nArguments\n\nequiCY: If this optional argument with default value false is set to true, the result is linearized   to be equivariantly Calabi-Yau. Note that the underlying 3d closed vertex geometry is not taken to be   equivariantly Calabi-Yau.\n\nExample\n\nLet us see the output of this function once with equiCY=false (default) and once with equiCY=true.\n\njulia> gkm_5d_closed_vertex()\nGKM graph with 4 nodes, valency 5 and axial function:\n0,1 -> 1,1 => (-1//2, 1//2, 1//2, 0, 0)\n2,1 -> 0,1 => (-1//2, 1//2, -1//2, 0, 0)\n3,1 -> 0,1 => (-1//2, -1//2, 1//2, 0, 0)\nStandalone flags:\n1,1.2 => (0, 1, 0, 0, 0)\n1,1.3 => (0, 0, 1, 0, 0)\n1,1.4 => (0, 0, 0, 1, 0)\n1,1.5 => (0, 0, 0, 0, 1)\n0,1.4 => (0, 0, 0, 1, 0)\n0,1.5 => (0, 0, 0, 0, 1)\n2,1.2 => (1, 0, 0, 0, 0)\n2,1.3 => (0, 0, 1, 0, 0)\n2,1.4 => (0, 0, 0, 1, 0)\n2,1.5 => (0, 0, 0, 0, 1)\n3,1.2 => (1, 0, 0, 0, 0)\n3,1.3 => (0, 1, 0, 0, 0)\n3,1.4 => (0, 0, 0, 1, 0)\n3,1.5 => (0, 0, 0, 0, 1)\n\njulia> gkm_5d_closed_vertex(equiCY=true)\nGKM graph with 4 nodes, valency 5 and axial function:\n0,1 -> 1,1 => (-1, 0, 0, -1, -1)\n2,1 -> 0,1 => (0, 1, 0, 1, 1)\n3,1 -> 0,1 => (-1, -1, 0, -1, -1)\nStandalone flags:\n1,1.2 => (0, 1, 0, 0, 0)\n1,1.3 => (-1, -1, 0, -2, -2)\n1,1.4 => (0, 0, 0, 1, 0)\n1,1.5 => (0, 0, 0, 0, 1)\n0,1.4 => (0, 0, 0, 1, 0)\n0,1.5 => (0, 0, 0, 0, 1)\n2,1.2 => (1, 0, 0, 0, 0)\n2,1.3 => (-1, -1, 0, -2, -2)\n2,1.4 => (0, 0, 0, 1, 0)\n2,1.5 => (0, 0, 0, 0, 1)\n3,1.2 => (1, 0, 0, 0, 0)\n3,1.3 => (0, 1, 0, 0, 0)\n3,1.4 => (0, 0, 0, 1, 0)\n3,1.5 => (0, 0, 0, 0, 1)\n\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GW_CY5.gkm_5d_closed_vertex"},{"category":"function","location":"spaces/#GW_CY5.gkm_3d_gauge","page":"Spaces","text":"gkm_3d_gauge(N::Int64, m::Int64)\n\nReturn the GKM graph of the CY3 space X_Nm from [HS26, Section 3.1]. The returned space is Calabi-Yau but not equivariantly Calabi-Yau.\n\nThis function is used by gkm_5d_gauge to construct the GKM graph of Z_Nm = X_NmtimesmathbbC^2.\n\nExample\n\njulia> G = gkm_3d_gauge(3, 5)\nGKM graph with 6 nodes, valency 3 and axial function:\nv2 -> v1 => (1, 1, 0)\nv3 -> v2 => (1, 2, 0)\nw1 -> v1 => (0, 0, -1)\nw2 -> v2 => (0, 0, -1)\nw2 -> w1 => (1, 1, 6)\nw3 -> v3 => (0, 0, -1)\nw3 -> w2 => (1, 2, 4)\nStandalone flags:\nv1.2 => (1, 0, 0)\nv3.3 => (-1, -3, 0)\nw1.2 => (1, 0, 8)\nw3.3 => (-1, -3, -2)\n\njulia> print_curve_classes(G)\nv2 -> v1: (1, 0, 0), Chern number: 0\nv3 -> v2: (0, 0, 1), Chern number: 0\nw1 -> v1: (6, 1, 4), Chern number: 0\nw2 -> v2: (0, 1, 4), Chern number: 0\nw2 -> w1: (1, 0, 0), Chern number: 0\nw3 -> v3: (0, 1, 0), Chern number: 0\nw3 -> w2: (0, 0, 1), Chern number: 0\n\njulia> first_chern_class(G)\n(-t2 + t3)*e[1] + (-t2 + t3)*e[2] + (-t2 + t3)*e[3] + (-t2 + t3)*e[4] + (-t2 + t3)*e[5] + (-t2 + t3)*e[6]\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GW_CY5.gkm_3d_gauge"},{"category":"function","location":"spaces/#GW_CY5.gkm_5d_gauge","page":"Spaces","text":"gkm_5d_gauge(N::Int64, m::Int64; equiCY::Bool=false)\n\nReturn the GKM graph of the CY5 space Z_Nm from [HS26, Section 3.1]. If the optional argument equiCY is set to true then the substitution epsilon_2=epsilon_3+epsilon_4+epsilon_5 is applied to make the result equivariantly Calabi-Yau.\n\nExamples\n\nLet us first see the non-equivariantly Calabi-Yau example.\n\njulia> G = gkm_5d_gauge(3, 5)\nGKM graph with 6 nodes, valency 5 and axial function:\nv2 -> v1 => (1, 1, 0, 0, 0)\nv3 -> v2 => (1, 2, 0, 0, 0)\nw1 -> v1 => (0, 0, -1, 0, 0)\nw2 -> v2 => (0, 0, -1, 0, 0)\nw2 -> w1 => (1, 1, 6, 0, 0)\nw3 -> v3 => (0, 0, -1, 0, 0)\nw3 -> w2 => (1, 2, 4, 0, 0)\nStandalone flags:\nv1.2 => (1, 0, 0, 0, 0)\nv1.4 => (0, 0, 0, 1, 0)\nv1.5 => (0, 0, 0, 0, 1)\nv2.4 => (0, 0, 0, 1, 0)\nv2.5 => (0, 0, 0, 0, 1)\nv3.3 => (-1, -3, 0, 0, 0)\nv3.4 => (0, 0, 0, 1, 0)\nv3.5 => (0, 0, 0, 0, 1)\nw1.2 => (1, 0, 8, 0, 0)\nw1.4 => (0, 0, 0, 1, 0)\nw1.5 => (0, 0, 0, 0, 1)\nw2.4 => (0, 0, 0, 1, 0)\nw2.5 => (0, 0, 0, 0, 1)\nw3.3 => (-1, -3, -2, 0, 0)\nw3.4 => (0, 0, 0, 1, 0)\nw3.5 => (0, 0, 0, 0, 1)\n\njulia> first_chern_class(G)\n(-t2 + t3 + t4 + t5)*e[1] + (-t2 + t3 + t4 + t5)*e[2] + (-t2 + t3 + t4 + t5)*e[3] + (-t2 + t3 + t4 + t5)*e[4] + (-t2 + t3 + t4 + t5)*e[5] + (-t2 + t3 + t4 + t5)*e[6]\n\nSecond, let us see the equivariantly Calabi-Yau example.\n\njulia> G = gkm_5d_gauge(3, 5; equiCY=true)\nGKM graph with 6 nodes, valency 5 and axial function:\nv2 -> v1 => (1, 0, 1, 1, 1)\nv3 -> v2 => (1, 0, 2, 2, 2)\nw1 -> v1 => (0, 0, -1, 0, 0)\nw2 -> v2 => (0, 0, -1, 0, 0)\nw2 -> w1 => (1, 0, 7, 1, 1)\nw3 -> v3 => (0, 0, -1, 0, 0)\nw3 -> w2 => (1, 0, 6, 2, 2)\nStandalone flags:\nv1.2 => (1, 0, 0, 0, 0)\nv1.4 => (0, 0, 0, 1, 0)\nv1.5 => (0, 0, 0, 0, 1)\nv2.4 => (0, 0, 0, 1, 0)\nv2.5 => (0, 0, 0, 0, 1)\nv3.3 => (-1, 0, -3, -3, -3)\nv3.4 => (0, 0, 0, 1, 0)\nv3.5 => (0, 0, 0, 0, 1)\nw1.2 => (1, 0, 8, 0, 0)\nw1.4 => (0, 0, 0, 1, 0)\nw1.5 => (0, 0, 0, 0, 1)\nw2.4 => (0, 0, 0, 1, 0)\nw2.5 => (0, 0, 0, 0, 1)\nw3.3 => (-1, 0, -5, -3, -3)\nw3.4 => (0, 0, 0, 1, 0)\nw3.5 => (0, 0, 0, 0, 1)\n\njulia> first_chern_class(G)\n0\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GW_CY5.gkm_5d_gauge"},{"category":"function","location":"spaces/#GW_CY5.X_times_Ar","page":"Spaces","text":"X_times_Ar(X::AbstractGKM_graph, r::Int64; equiCY::Bool=false) -> GKMtools.AbstractGKM_graph\n\nReturn XtimesmathcalA_r.\n\nArguments\n\nX: The GKM graph of a 3-fold.\nr: The number of T-stable rational curves in mathcalA_r.\nequiCY: If this optional argument with default value false is set to true, the returned space will be linearized to be equivariantly Calabi-Yau.\n\nwarning: Warning\nFor equiCY=true, this function requires X to be Calabi-Yau, although not necessarily equivariantly.\n\nExample\n\nLet us produce the example mathcalA_2timesmathbbCtimesmathcalA_3.\n\njulia> A2_times_C = Ar_times_C1(2)\nGKM graph with 3 nodes, valency 3 and axial function:\n2 -> 1 => (1, -1, 0)\n3 -> 2 => (1, -2, 0)\nStandalone flags:\n1.2 => (0, 0, 1)\n1.3 => (1, 0, 0)\n2.3 => (0, 0, 1)\n3.2 => (0, 0, 1)\n3.3 => (-1, 3, 0)\n\njulia> A2_times_A3 = X_times_Ar(A2_times_C, 3)\nGKM graph with 12 nodes, valency 5 and axial function:\n2,1 -> 1,1 => (1, -1, 0, 0, 0)\n3,1 -> 2,1 => (1, -2, 0, 0, 0)\n1,2 -> 1,1 => (0, 0, 0, 1, -1)\n2,2 -> 2,1 => (0, 0, 0, 1, -1)\n2,2 -> 1,2 => (1, -1, 0, 0, 0)\n3,2 -> 3,1 => (0, 0, 0, 1, -1)\n3,2 -> 2,2 => (1, -2, 0, 0, 0)\n1,3 -> 1,2 => (0, 0, 0, 1, -2)\n2,3 -> 2,2 => (0, 0, 0, 1, -2)\n2,3 -> 1,3 => (1, -1, 0, 0, 0)\n3,3 -> 3,2 => (0, 0, 0, 1, -2)\n3,3 -> 2,3 => (1, -2, 0, 0, 0)\n1,4 -> 1,3 => (0, 0, 0, 1, -3)\n2,4 -> 2,3 => (0, 0, 0, 1, -3)\n2,4 -> 1,4 => (1, -1, 0, 0, 0)\n3,4 -> 3,3 => (0, 0, 0, 1, -3)\n3,4 -> 2,4 => (1, -2, 0, 0, 0)\nStandalone flags:\n1,1.2 => (0, 0, 1, 0, 0)\n1,1.3 => (1, 0, 0, 0, 0)\n1,1.5 => (0, 0, 0, 1, 0)\n2,1.3 => (0, 0, 1, 0, 0)\n2,1.5 => (0, 0, 0, 1, 0)\n3,1.2 => (0, 0, 1, 0, 0)\n3,1.3 => (-1, 3, 0, 0, 0)\n3,1.5 => (0, 0, 0, 1, 0)\n1,2.2 => (0, 0, 1, 0, 0)\n1,2.3 => (1, 0, 0, 0, 0)\n2,2.3 => (0, 0, 1, 0, 0)\n3,2.2 => (0, 0, 1, 0, 0)\n3,2.3 => (-1, 3, 0, 0, 0)\n1,3.2 => (0, 0, 1, 0, 0)\n1,3.3 => (1, 0, 0, 0, 0)\n2,3.3 => (0, 0, 1, 0, 0)\n3,3.2 => (0, 0, 1, 0, 0)\n3,3.3 => (-1, 3, 0, 0, 0)\n1,4.2 => (0, 0, 1, 0, 0)\n1,4.3 => (1, 0, 0, 0, 0)\n1,4.5 => (0, 0, 0, -1, 4)\n2,4.3 => (0, 0, 1, 0, 0)\n2,4.5 => (0, 0, 0, -1, 4)\n3,4.2 => (0, 0, 1, 0, 0)\n3,4.3 => (-1, 3, 0, 0, 0)\n3,4.5 => (0, 0, 0, -1, 4)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GW_CY5.X_times_Ar"},{"category":"function","location":"spaces/#GW_CY5.CY5_from_CY4","page":"Spaces","text":"CY5_from_CY4(CY4::AbstractGKM_graph; equiCY::Bool=false)\n\nReturn XtimesmathbbC^1.\n\nArguments\n\nX: The GKM graph of a 4-fold.\nequiCY: If this optional argument with default value false is set to true, the returned space will be linearized to be equivariantly Calabi-Yau.\n\nwarning: Warning\nFor equiCY=true, this function requires X to be Calabi-Yau, although not necessarily equivariantly.\n\nExample\n\nLet us consider the example with X=textTot(mathcalO_mathbbP^2(-1)oplus mathcalO_mathbbP^2(-2)).\n\njulia> X = total_space(vector_bundle_O(2, [-1, -2]))\nGKM graph with 3 nodes, valency 4 and axial function:\n2 -> 1 => (-1, 1, 0, 0, 0)\n3 -> 1 => (-1, 0, 1, 0, 0)\n3 -> 2 => (0, -1, 1, 0, 0)\nStandalone flags:\n1.3 => (0, 0, 0, 1, 0)\n1.4 => (0, 0, 0, 0, 1)\n2.3 => (1, -1, 0, 1, 0)\n2.4 => (2, -2, 0, 0, 1)\n3.3 => (1, 0, -1, 1, 0)\n3.4 => (2, 0, -2, 0, 1)\n\njulia> CY5_from_CY4(X)\nGKM graph with 3 nodes, valency 5 and axial function:\n2,1 -> 1,1 => (-1, 1, 0, 0, 0, 0)\n3,1 -> 1,1 => (-1, 0, 1, 0, 0, 0)\n3,1 -> 2,1 => (0, -1, 1, 0, 0, 0)\nStandalone flags:\n1,1.3 => (0, 0, 0, 1, 0, 0)\n1,1.4 => (0, 0, 0, 0, 1, 0)\n1,1.5 => (0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 1)\n2,1.3 => (1, -1, 0, 1, 0, 0)\n2,1.4 => (2, -2, 0, 0, 1, 0)\n2,1.5 => (0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 1)\n3,1.3 => (1, 0, -1, 1, 0, 0)\n3,1.4 => (2, 0, -2, 0, 1, 0)\n3,1.5 => (0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 1)\n\njulia> CY5_from_CY4(X; equiCY=true) # Let us also see the equivariantly CY substitution.\nGKM graph with 3 nodes, valency 5 and axial function:\n2,1 -> 1,1 => (-1, 1, 0, 0, 0, 0)\n3,1 -> 1,1 => (-1, 0, 1, 0, 0, 0)\n3,1 -> 2,1 => (0, -1, 1, 0, 0, 0)\nStandalone flags:\n1,1.3 => (0, 0, 0, 1, 0, 0)\n1,1.4 => (0, 0, 0, 0, 1, 0)\n1,1.5 => (-2, 1, 1, -1, -1, 0)\n2,1.3 => (1, -1, 0, 1, 0, 0)\n2,1.4 => (2, -2, 0, 0, 1, 0)\n2,1.5 => (-2, 1, 1, -1, -1, 0)\n3,1.3 => (1, 0, -1, 1, 0, 0)\n3,1.4 => (2, 0, -2, 0, 1, 0)\n3,1.5 => (-2, 1, 1, -1, -1, 0)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GW_CY5.CY5_from_CY4"},{"category":"function","location":"spaces/#GW_CY5.gkm_5d_P2_111","page":"Spaces","text":"gkm_5d_P2_111()\n\nReturn the GKM graph of textTotmathcalO_mathbbP^2(-1)^oplus 3 with the specific equivariantly CY linearization as in the example below.\n\nExample\n\njulia> G = gkm_5d_P2_111()\nGKM graph with 3 nodes, valency 5 and axial function:\n2 -> 1 => (2, -2, 0, 0, 0)\n3 -> 1 => (2, 0, -2, 0, 0)\n3 -> 2 => (0, 2, -2, 0, 0)\nStandalone flags:\n1.3 => (2, 0, 0, 2, 0)\n1.4 => (2, 0, 0, 0, 2)\n1.5 => (0, -2, -2, -2, -2)\n2.3 => (0, 2, 0, 2, 0)\n2.4 => (0, 2, 0, 0, 2)\n2.5 => (-2, 0, -2, -2, -2)\n3.3 => (0, 0, 2, 2, 0)\n3.4 => (0, 0, 2, 0, 2)\n3.5 => (-2, -2, 0, -2, -2)\n\njulia> first_chern_class(G)\n0\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GW_CY5.gkm_5d_P2_111"},{"category":"function","location":"spaces/#GW_CY5.gkm_5d_P3_04","page":"Spaces","text":"gkm_5d_P3_04()\n\nReturn the GKM graph of textTot(mathcalO_mathbbP^3(0)oplus mathcalO_mathbbP^3(-4)) with the specific equivariantly CY linearization as in the example below.\n\nExample\n\njulia> G = gkm_5d_P3_04()\nGKM graph with 4 nodes, valency 5 and axial function:\n2 -> 1 => (2, -2, 0, 0, 0)\n3 -> 1 => (2, 0, -2, 0, 0)\n3 -> 2 => (0, 2, -2, 0, 0)\n4 -> 1 => (2, 0, 0, -2, 0)\n4 -> 2 => (0, 2, 0, -2, 0)\n4 -> 3 => (0, 0, 2, -2, 0)\nStandalone flags:\n1.4 => (0, 0, 0, 0, 2)\n1.5 => (6, -2, -2, -2, -2)\n2.4 => (0, 0, 0, 0, 2)\n2.5 => (-2, 6, -2, -2, -2)\n3.4 => (0, 0, 0, 0, 2)\n3.5 => (-2, -2, 6, -2, -2)\n4.4 => (0, 0, 0, 0, 2)\n4.5 => (-2, -2, -2, 6, -2)\n\njulia> first_chern_class(G)\n0\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GW_CY5.gkm_5d_P3_04"},{"category":"function","location":"spaces/#GW_CY5.gkm_5d_P3_13","page":"Spaces","text":"gkm_5d_P3_13()\n\nReturn the GKM graph of textTot(mathcalO_mathbbP^3(-1)oplus mathcalO_mathbbP^3(-3)) with the specific equivariantly CY linearization as in the example below.\n\nExample\n\njulia> G = gkm_5d_P3_13()\nGKM graph with 4 nodes, valency 5 and axial function:\n2 -> 1 => (2, -2, 0, 0, 0)\n3 -> 1 => (2, 0, -2, 0, 0)\n3 -> 2 => (0, 2, -2, 0, 0)\n4 -> 1 => (2, 0, 0, -2, 0)\n4 -> 2 => (0, 2, 0, -2, 0)\n4 -> 3 => (0, 0, 2, -2, 0)\nStandalone flags:\n1.4 => (2, 0, 0, 0, 2)\n1.5 => (4, -2, -2, -2, -2)\n2.4 => (0, 2, 0, 0, 2)\n2.5 => (-2, 4, -2, -2, -2)\n3.4 => (0, 0, 2, 0, 2)\n3.5 => (-2, -2, 4, -2, -2)\n4.4 => (0, 0, 0, 2, 2)\n4.5 => (-2, -2, -2, 4, -2)\n\njulia> first_chern_class(G)\n0\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GW_CY5.gkm_5d_P3_13"},{"category":"function","location":"spaces/#GW_CY5.gkm_5d_P3_22","page":"Spaces","text":"gkm_5d_P3_22()\n\nReturn the GKM graph of textTotmathcalO_mathbbP^3(-2)^oplus 2 with the specific equivariantly CY linearization as in the example below.\n\nExample\n\njulia> G = gkm_5d_P3_22()\nGKM graph with 4 nodes, valency 5 and axial function:\n2 -> 1 => (2, -2, 0, 0, 0)\n3 -> 1 => (2, 0, -2, 0, 0)\n3 -> 2 => (0, 2, -2, 0, 0)\n4 -> 1 => (2, 0, 0, -2, 0)\n4 -> 2 => (0, 2, 0, -2, 0)\n4 -> 3 => (0, 0, 2, -2, 0)\nStandalone flags:\n1.4 => (4, 0, 0, 0, 2)\n1.5 => (2, -2, -2, -2, -2)\n2.4 => (0, 4, 0, 0, 2)\n2.5 => (-2, 2, -2, -2, -2)\n3.4 => (0, 0, 4, 0, 2)\n3.5 => (-2, -2, 2, -2, -2)\n4.4 => (0, 0, 0, 4, 2)\n4.5 => (-2, -2, -2, 2, -2)\n\njulia> first_chern_class(G)\n0\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GW_CY5.gkm_5d_P3_22"},{"category":"section","location":"pt/pt_cy5_overview/#PT_CY5.sage","page":"Overview","text":"A SageMath package for computing K-theoretic PT invariants with Nekrasov-Okounkov insertions.\n\nWe build on the SageMath package boxcounting [Liu25] by Henry Liu to compute K-theoretic Pandharipande-Thomas invariants of toric threefolds with a Nekrasov-Okounkov insertion.","title":"PT_CY5.sage"},{"category":"section","location":"pt/pt_cy5_overview/#Contents","page":"Overview","text":"Installation instructions\nUsage instructions/examples from the paper\nEvidence: for each numerical PT experiment cited as Evidence in the paper, we provide code to reproduce the stated results.","title":"Contents"},{"category":"section","location":"pt/pt_cy5_overview/#pt_installation","page":"Overview","text":"First, install SageMath following the instructions here. After that, download this GitHub project. Navigate to the folder CY5/pt/. Next, download all files from Liu's boxcounting package and place them into a folder CY5/pt/boxcounting/. This should result in the following folder structure:\n\nCY5/\n├── pt/\n│   ├── boxcounting/            # Liu's boxcounting package\n│   │   ├── bare_vertex.sage\n│   │   ├── edge.sage\n│   │   ├── pt_configuration.sage\n│   │   ├── setup.sage\n│   │   └── ...\n│   ├── PT_CY5.sage\n│   └── ...\n├── test/\n│   ├── omega_data/             # Membrane index formulae\n│   └── ...\n└── ...\n\nwarning: Warning\nIf the folder omega_data containing conjectural formulae for membrane indices is moved to a different place, the string omega_data_folder in local_P2.sage and local_P3.sage has to be updated accordingly for the scripts to run.","title":"Installation"},{"category":"section","location":"code_from_paper/#Julia-code-in-the-paper","page":"Julia code in the paper","text":"On this page, we mirror the Julia code presented in the paper.","title":"Julia code in the paper"},{"category":"section","location":"code_from_paper/#Example-1.1","page":"Julia code in the paper","text":"In [HS26, Example 1.1], the GKM graph G of Z = textTot_mathbbP^1(mathcalO(-2))times mathbbC^3 with a certain torus action is introduced. The following function can be used to obtain its GKM graph.","title":"Example 1.1"},{"category":"section","location":"code_from_paper/#Example-1.2","page":"Julia code in the paper","text":"In [HS26, Example 1.2], we compute the Gromov-Witten of Z in degrees 123 and genus 0 and 1 using the following code.\n\njulia> using Oscar, GKMtools, GW_CY5\n\njulia> G = gkm_graph_of_example_1_1();\n\njulia> beta = curve_class(G, Edge(1, 2));\n\njulia> gMax = 1;\n\njulia> GW = get_GW_beta(G, [beta, 2*beta, 3*beta], gMax; show_bar=false);\nCalculating b=(1), g=0\nCalculating b=(1), g=1\nCalculating b=(2), g=0\nCalculating b=(2), g=1\nCalculating b=(3), g=0\nCalculating b=(3), g=1\n\njulia> GW[beta]\n(1//12*t1*t4*t5*u^2 + 1//12*t1*t4*t6*u^2 + 1//12*t1*t5*t6*u^2 + t1 + 1//12*t2*t4*t5*u^2 + 1//12*t2*t4*t6*u^2 + 1//12*t2*t5*t6*u^2 + t2 + 1//12*t3*t4*t5*u^2 + 1//12*t3*t4*t6*u^2 + 1//12*t3*t5*t6*u^2 + t3)//(t4*t5*t6*u^2)\n\njulia> GW[2*beta]\n(1//24*t1*t4*t5*u^2 + 1//24*t1*t4*t6*u^2 + 1//24*t1*t5*t6*u^2 + 1//8*t1 + 1//24*t2*t4*t5*u^2 + 1//24*t2*t4*t6*u^2 + 1//24*t2*t5*t6*u^2 + 1//8*t2 + 1//24*t3*t4*t5*u^2 + 1//24*t3*t4*t6*u^2 + 1//24*t3*t5*t6*u^2 + 1//8*t3)//(t4*t5*t6*u^2)\n\njulia> GW[3*beta]\n(1//36*t1*t4*t5*u^2 + 1//36*t1*t4*t6*u^2 + 1//36*t1*t5*t6*u^2 + 1//27*t1 + 1//36*t2*t4*t5*u^2 + 1//36*t2*t4*t6*u^2 + 1//36*t2*t5*t6*u^2 + 1//27*t2 + 1//36*t3*t4*t5*u^2 + 1//36*t3*t4*t6*u^2 + 1//36*t3*t5*t6*u^2 + 1//27*t3)//(t4*t5*t6*u^2)","title":"Example 1.2"},{"category":"section","location":"code_from_paper/#Example-1.4","page":"Julia code in the paper","text":"Finally, in [HS26, Example 1.4] we compute Omega_beta for the same example and in the same range of degree and genus. We do this by continuing the above code as follows.\n\njulia> Omega = get_Omega_beta(G, [beta, 2*beta, 3*beta], gMax; show_bar=false);\nCalculating b=(1), g=0\nCalculating b=(1), g=1\nCalculating b=(2), g=0\nCalculating b=(2), g=1\nCalculating b=(3), g=0\nCalculating b=(3), g=1\n\njulia> Omega[beta]\n(1//12*t1*t4*t5*u^2 + 1//12*t1*t4*t6*u^2 + 1//12*t1*t5*t6*u^2 + t1 + 1//12*t2*t4*t5*u^2 + 1//12*t2*t4*t6*u^2 + 1//12*t2*t5*t6*u^2 + t2 + 1//12*t3*t4*t5*u^2 + 1//12*t3*t4*t6*u^2 + 1//12*t3*t5*t6*u^2 + t3)//(t4*t5*t6*u^2)\n\njulia> Omega[2*beta]\n0\n\njulia> Omega[3*beta]\n0","title":"Example 1.4"},{"category":"function","location":"code_from_paper/#GW_CY5.gkm_graph_of_example_1_1","page":"Julia code in the paper","text":"gkm_graph_of_example_1_1()\n\nReturn the GKM graph of textTot_mathbbP^1(mathcalO(-2))timesmathbbC^3 as defined in [HS26, Example 1.1].\n\nExample\n\njulia> G = gkm_graph_of_example_1_1()\nGKM graph with 2 nodes, valency 5 and axial function:\n2 -> 1 => (1, -1, 0, 0, 0, 0)\nStandalone flags:\n1.2 => (2, 0, 1, 0, 0, 0)\n1.3 => (0, 0, 0, 1, 0, 0)\n1.4 => (0, 0, 0, 0, 1, 0)\n1.5 => (0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 1)\n2.2 => (0, 2, 1, 0, 0, 0)\n2.3 => (0, 0, 0, 1, 0, 0)\n2.4 => (0, 0, 0, 0, 1, 0)\n2.5 => (0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 1)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GW_CY5.gkm_graph_of_example_1_1"},{"category":"section","location":"pt/pt_example/#Usage-instructions/examples-from-the-paper","page":"Example","text":"For everyone interested in using our code we refer to the Jupyter notebook CY5\\pt\\exmaples.ipynb for two worked out examples from our paper.\n\nWe also wrote a Jupyter notebook for our check of [HS26, Evidence 2.12] concerning the closed vertex containing multiple comments. It is found under  CY5\\pt\\closed_vertex.ipynb.","title":"Usage instructions/examples from the paper"},{"category":"section","location":"conj/3d_strip_xC2/#Evidence-2.8","page":"X_text3d-striptimesmathbbC^2 (Evidence 2.8)","text":"We provide evidence for [HS26, Conjecture 2.4] which concerns T-spaces of the form XtimesmathbbC^2 where X is a 3d strip geometry.","title":"Evidence 2.8"},{"category":"section","location":"conj/3d_strip_xC2/#\\mathcal{A}_3\\times-\\mathbb{C}3","page":"X_text3d-striptimesmathbbC^2 (Evidence 2.8)","text":"The following Julia code verifies [HS26, Conjecture 2.4] for mathcalA_rtimes mathbbC^3 with r=3 up to genus 3.\n\nThe curve classes b,c,d correspond to the three T-stable rational curves  denoted and are denoted beta_1beta_2beta_3 in the paper. Note that get_Omega_beta checks all curve classes passed to it in the second argument, as well as all curve classes whose multiples appear in that list. For example, the command get_Omega_beta(G, [6*b], max_genus; check_predictions=true) checks the conjecture for b, 2*b, 3*b, 6*b (see Pipeline for more details).\n\nUnder each line calling get_Omega_beta, we recorded the crucial line of the output as a comment.\n\nusing Markdown\nMarkdown.parse(\"```julia\\n\" * read(joinpath(@__DIR__, \"..\", \"..\", \"..\", \"test\", \"results\", \"Ar_C3.jl\"), String) * \"\\n```\")","title":"mathcalA_3times mathbbC^3"},{"category":"section","location":"conj/3d_strip_xC2/#X_3{(-1,-1)}\\times\\mathbb{C}2","page":"X_text3d-striptimesmathbbC^2 (Evidence 2.8)","text":"The following Julia code checks the conjecture up to genus 2 for XtimesmathbbC^2 where X=X_3^(-1-1) is the 3d strip geometry given by minus_one_minus_one_chain, that is, a chain of 3 local mathbbP^1's.\n\nUnder each line calling get_Omega_beta, we recorded the crucial line of the output as a comment.\n\nusing Markdown\nMarkdown.parse(\"```julia\\n\" * read(joinpath(@__DIR__, \"..\", \"..\", \"..\", \"test\", \"results\", \"minus_1_minus_1_chain.jl\"), String) * \"\\n```\")","title":"X_3^(-1-1)timesmathbbC^2"},{"category":"section","location":"conj/3d_strip_xC2/#A-mixed-example","page":"X_text3d-striptimesmathbbC^2 (Evidence 2.8)","text":"Finally, the following Julia code verifies [HS26, Conjecture 2.4] up to genus 3 for the mixed example introduced in [HS26, Evidence 2.8]. Under each line calling get_Omega_beta, we recorded the crucial line of the output as a comment.\n\nusing Markdown\nMarkdown.parse(\"```julia\\n\" * read(joinpath(@__DIR__, \"..\", \"..\", \"..\", \"test\", \"results\", \"mixed_3d_strip.jl\"), String) * \"\\n```\")","title":"A mixed example"},{"category":"section","location":"conj/cy5_from_cy4/#Evidence-5.5","page":"Local mathbbP^2, mathbbP^1timesmathbbP^1 (Evidence 5.5)","text":"On this page, we provide Julia code to verify the vanishing of certain Gromov-Witten invariants conjectured in [HS26, Conjecture 5.1]. The corresponding GKM graphs are obtained using CY5_from_CY4. After each line calling get_Omega_beta, we added the crucial line of the output as a comment.","title":"Evidence 5.5"},{"category":"section","location":"conj/cy5_from_cy4/#\\mathcal{O}(-1)\\oplus-\\mathcal{O}(-2)","page":"Local mathbbP^2, mathbbP^1timesmathbbP^1 (Evidence 5.5)","text":"This example concerns the total space of mathcalO(-1)oplus mathcalO(-2)oplusmathcalO on mathbbP^2. The curve calss b in the code corresponds to H in the paper.\n\nusing Markdown\nMarkdown.parse(\"```julia\\n\" * read(joinpath(@__DIR__, \"..\", \"..\", \"..\", \"test\", \"results\", \"CY4_vanishing\", \"P2.jl\"), String) * \"\\n```\")","title":"mathcalO(-1)oplus mathcalO(-2)"},{"category":"section","location":"conj/cy5_from_cy4/#\\mathcal{O}(-1,-1)\\oplus-\\mathcal{O}(-1,-1)","page":"Local mathbbP^2, mathbbP^1timesmathbbP^1 (Evidence 5.5)","text":"This example concerns the total space of mathcalO(-1 -1)oplus mathcalO(-1 -1)oplusmathcalO on mathbbP^1timesmathbbP^1. The curve classes b,c in the code correspond to (1 0) and (0 1) in the paper.\n\nusing Markdown\nMarkdown.parse(\"```julia\\n\" * read(joinpath(@__DIR__, \"..\", \"..\", \"..\", \"test\", \"results\", \"CY4_vanishing\", \"P1_P1_1_1.jl\"), String) * \"\\n```\")","title":"mathcalO(-1 -1)oplus mathcalO(-1 -1)"},{"category":"section","location":"conj/cy5_from_cy4/#\\mathcal{O}(-2,-0)\\oplus-\\mathcal{O}(0,-2)","page":"Local mathbbP^2, mathbbP^1timesmathbbP^1 (Evidence 5.5)","text":"This example concerns the total space of mathcalO(-2 0)oplus mathcalO(0 -2)oplusmathcalO on mathbbP^1timesmathbbP^1. The curve classes b,c in the code correspond to (1 0) and (0 1) in the paper.\n\nusing Markdown\nMarkdown.parse(\"```julia\\n\" * read(joinpath(@__DIR__, \"..\", \"..\", \"..\", \"test\", \"results\", \"CY4_vanishing\", \"P1_P1_0_2.jl\"), String) * \"\\n```\")\n\nThe final line of code does not contradict [HS26, Conjecture 5.1]. Instead, it confirms that the properness assumption of the conjecture is necessary.","title":"mathcalO(-2 0)oplus mathcalO(0 -2)"},{"category":"section","location":"conj/overview/#Overview-of-GW-evidence","page":"Overview of GW evidence","text":"In this section, we explain how our GW calculations cited as Evidence in [HS26] can be reproduced.\n\nEvidence Space Main function(s)\nEvidence 2.8 XtimesmathbbC^2 where X is a 3d strip geometry gkm_5d_strip\nEvidence 2.13 XtimesmathbbC^2 where X is the closed vertex gkm_5d_closed_vertex\nEvidence 3.5 X_NmtimesmathbbC^2 (GW-gauge correspondence) gkm_5d_gauge\nEvidence 4.2 textTot(mathcalO_mathbbP^2(-1)^oplus 3) gkm_5d_P2_111\nEvidence 4.8 textTot(mathcalO_mathbbP^3(-a)oplusmathcalO_mathbbP^3(a-4)) gkm_5d_P3_04/_13/_22\nEvidence 5.5 mathbbCtimesleft(textTotmathcalO_S(-D_1)oplus mathcalO_S(-D_1)right) CY5_from_CY4\n\nIn the last row, we treat the cases S=mathbbP^2 with mathcalO(-1)oplusmathcalO(-2) and S=mathbbP^1times mathbbP^1 with mathcalO(-1-1)oplusmathcalO(-1-1) and mathcalO(-2 0)oplusmathcalO(0 -2).\n\nnote: Note\nNote that we executed most of the code listed in this section on a compute cluster provided by the Mathematics Department of ETH Zürich with the following metrics.Operating System: Fedora\nCPU: 2x Intel Xeon Gold 6254 (18 Cores) 3.1 GHz\nRAM: 512 GBTherefore, it may be hard to reproduce some of our examples on an ordinary PC.","title":"Overview of GW evidence"},{"category":"section","location":"pipeline/#Pipeline","page":"Pipeline","text":"On this page, we explain how to analyze a given CY5 space.","title":"Pipeline"},{"category":"section","location":"pipeline/#Mathematical-setup","page":"Pipeline","text":"We recall the mathematical setup of [HS26], to which we refer for full details. Let X be a smooth equivariantly Calabi-Yau GKM space with respect to the torus T, and let epsilon_1dotsepsilon_r be the equivariant parameters for T. Given a curve class beta and genus gge 0, let\n\nGW_gbeta(XT) in mathbbQ(epsilon_1dotsepsilon_r)\n\nbe the associated T-equivariant Gromov-Witten invariant (with connected domain curves) for X in genus g and curve class beta. Let u and Q be formal variables. Then\n\nOmega_beta(epsilon u)in mathbbQ(epsilon_1dotsepsilon_r u)\n\nis defined by\n\nsum_betaneq 0sum_gge 0 Q^beta u^2g-2  GW_gbeta(XT)\n=\nsum_betaneq 0\nsum_k0 frac1k Q^kbeta Omega_beta(epsilonku)\n\nThe point of this package is to test conjectural formulae for Omega_beta in specific examples.\n\nnote: Note\nThe conjectural formulae for Omega_beta are formulated in terms of the substitution q_i=e^uepsilon_i. As GKMtools.jl computes GW_gbeta(XT) using the equivariant parameters epsilon_i, we only perform this substitution at the end when we compare the conjectural formula to the computed low-order terms of Omega_beta in u.","title":"Mathematical setup"},{"category":"section","location":"pipeline/#Implementation","page":"Pipeline","text":"Our pipeline has the following structure.\n\nDefine the T-space X using GKMtools.jl.\nDefine the conjectured values of Omega_beta.\nCompute GW_gbeta(XT) using GKMtools.jl for small values of gbeta.\nSolve the above recursion to compute Omega_beta up to some order of u.\nCompare the results.\n\nSteps 1. and 2. are implemented as one function for each family of spaces that we check in the paper (see Spaces). Steps 3.-5. are implemented in the function get_Omega_beta below.","title":"Implementation"},{"category":"section","location":"pipeline/#The-main-function","page":"Pipeline","text":"The following function is the main function used in our numerical experiments to confirm conjectural values of Omega_beta up to a certain genus.","title":"The main function"},{"category":"section","location":"pipeline/#Most-important-helper-functions","page":"Pipeline","text":"The two most important helper functions used by get_Omega_beta are:\n\nget_GW_beta: uses GKMtools.jl to compute individual Gromov-Witten invariants and packages them in a truncated generating series.\nget_Omega_prediction: provides the conjectured values of Omega_beta for comparison to the actual values computed from Gromov-Witten invariants.","title":"Most important helper functions"},{"category":"section","location":"pipeline/#More-details","page":"Pipeline","text":"A more detailed example of the pipeline in action can be found on the page Details of the Pipeline.","title":"More details"},{"category":"function","location":"pipeline/#GW_CY5.get_Omega_beta","page":"Pipeline","text":"get_Omega_beta(G::AbstractGKM_graph, betas::Vector, gMax::Int64; check_predictions::Bool=false)\n\nReturn a dictionary assigning to each curve class beta in betas the invariant Omega_beta as rational function in the equivariant parameters and the genus parameter u. Internally, this function uses get_GW_beta to get the Gromov-Witten invariants required to compute Omega_beta.\n\nThe output also contains Omega_beta for every beta for which some positive integer multiple is contained in beta.\n\nArguments\n\nG: the GKM graph of the space X whose Omega_beta should be computed.\nbetas: the vector of curve classes on X for which Omega_beta should be computed.\ngMax: the maximum genus up to which each Omega_beta will be approximated. That is,         the highest order term will be u^2textgMax-2.\ncheck_predictions: this optional argument with default value false controls whether the computed values of         Omega_beta are compared to the conjectured values. If check_predictions=true, the conjectured values         are retrieved from get_Omega_prediction.\nshow_bar: this optional argument can be set to false to disable the progress bar of the Gromov-Witten localization computation.\n\nExample 1: Computing Omega_beta\n\nLet us compute the genus zero and genus one terms of Omega_beta for the example Z=mathcalA_2times mathbbC^3 (see Spaces), for some examples of beta.\n\njulia> G = Ar_times_C3(2);\n\njulia> b = curve_class(G, Edge(1, 2))\n(1, 0)\n\njulia> c = curve_class(G, Edge(2, 3))\n(0, 1)\n\njulia> max_genus = 1;\n\njulia> Omega = get_Omega_beta(G, [3*b, 4*b, b+c], max_genus; show_bar=false);\nCalculating b=(3, 0), g=0\nCalculating b=(3, 0), g=1\nCalculating b=(1, 0), g=0\nCalculating b=(1, 0), g=1\nCalculating b=(4, 0), g=0\nCalculating b=(4, 0), g=1\nCalculating b=(2, 0), g=0\nCalculating b=(2, 0), g=1\nCalculating b=(1, 1), g=0\nCalculating b=(1, 1), g=1\n\njulia> Omega[b]\n(1//12*t2^2*t4*u^2 + 1//12*t2^2*t5*u^2 + 1//12*t2*t4^2*u^2 + 1//12*t2*t4*t5*u^2 + 1//12*t2*t5^2*u^2 - t2)//(t2*t4*t5*u^2 + t4^2*t5*u^2 + t4*t5^2*u^2)\n\njulia> Omega[2*b]\n0\n\njulia> Omega[3*b]\n0\n\njulia> Omega[4*b]\n0\n\njulia> Omega[b+c]\n(1//12*t2^2*t4*u^2 + 1//12*t2^2*t5*u^2 + 1//12*t2*t4^2*u^2 + 1//12*t2*t4*t5*u^2 + 1//12*t2*t5^2*u^2 - t2)//(t2*t4*t5*u^2 + t4^2*t5*u^2 + t4*t5^2*u^2)\n\nThe equivariant parameters epsilon_1epsilon_2dots are displayed as t1, t2, etc.\n\nExample 2: Checking conjectured values of Omega_beta\n\nSetting check_predictios=true, we can automatically check if Omega_beta matches the output of get_Omega_prediction:\n\njulia> get_Omega_beta(G, [3*b, 4*b, b+c], max_genus; show_bar=false, check_predictions=true);\nCalculating b=(3, 0), g=0\nCalculating b=(3, 0), g=1\nCalculating b=(1, 0), g=0\nCalculating b=(1, 0), g=1\nCalculating b=(4, 0), g=0\nCalculating b=(4, 0), g=1\nCalculating b=(2, 0), g=0\nCalculating b=(2, 0), g=1\nCalculating b=(1, 1), g=0\nCalculating b=(1, 1), g=1\nPrediction holds for (4, 0)\nPrediction holds for (2, 0)\nPrediction holds for (3, 0)\nPrediction holds for (1, 1)\nPrediction holds for (1, 0)\nAll predictions hold.\n\nIn this case, all predicticted values of Omega_beta hold in the tested range.\n\nExample 3: Behavior when the conjecture fails\n\nLet us also see an example in which the conjectured value of Omega_beta does not agree with the true value computed from Gromov-Witten invariants. We take\n\nZ=textTot_mathbbP^1timesmathbbP^1(mathcalO(-20)oplusmathcalO(0-2)oplusmathcalO)\n\nand let beta be the curve class of one of the two factor mathbbP^1s. Then the properness assumption of [HS26, Conjecture 5.1] does not hold for beta and its multiples. Let us denote beta by b in the following code.\n\njulia> P1_P1 = hirzebruch_surface(NormalToricVariety, 0)\nNormal toric variety\n\njulia> P = picard_group(P1_P1)\nZ^2\n\njulia> L1 = toric_line_bundle(P1_P1, -2*P[1]) # O(-2, 0)\nToric line bundle on a normal toric variety\n\njulia> L2 = toric_line_bundle(P1_P1, -2*P[2]) # O(0, -2)\nToric line bundle on a normal toric variety\n\njulia> CY4 = total_space(gkm_vector_bundle_of_toric([L1, L2]))\nGKM graph with 4 nodes, valency 4 and axial function:\n2 -> 1 => (1, 0, 0, 0, -1, 0)\n3 -> 2 => (0, 1, 0, 0, 0, -1)\n4 -> 1 => (0, 1, 0, 0, 0, -1)\n4 -> 3 => (-1, 0, 0, 0, 1, 0)\nStandalone flags:\n1.3 => (0, 0, -1, 0, -2, 0)\n1.4 => (0, 0, 0, -1, 0, -2)\n2.3 => (-2, 0, -1, 0, 0, 0)\n2.4 => (0, 0, 0, -1, 0, -2)\n3.3 => (-2, 0, -1, 0, 0, 0)\n3.4 => (0, -2, 0, -1, 0, 0)\n4.3 => (0, 0, -1, 0, -2, 0)\n4.4 => (0, -2, 0, -1, 0, 0)\n\njulia> G = CY5_from_CY4(CY4; equiCY=true)\nGKM graph with 4 nodes, valency 5 and axial function:\n2,1 -> 1,1 => (1, 0, 0, 0, -1, 0, 0)\n3,1 -> 2,1 => (0, 1, 0, 0, 0, -1, 0)\n4,1 -> 1,1 => (0, 1, 0, 0, 0, -1, 0)\n4,1 -> 3,1 => (-1, 0, 0, 0, 1, 0, 0)\nStandalone flags:\n1,1.3 => (0, 0, -1, 0, -2, 0, 0)\n1,1.4 => (0, 0, 0, -1, 0, -2, 0)\n1,1.5 => (1, 1, 1, 1, 1, 1, 0)\n2,1.3 => (-2, 0, -1, 0, 0, 0, 0)\n2,1.4 => (0, 0, 0, -1, 0, -2, 0)\n2,1.5 => (1, 1, 1, 1, 1, 1, 0)\n3,1.3 => (-2, 0, -1, 0, 0, 0, 0)\n3,1.4 => (0, -2, 0, -1, 0, 0, 0)\n3,1.5 => (1, 1, 1, 1, 1, 1, 0)\n4,1.3 => (0, 0, -1, 0, -2, 0, 0)\n4,1.4 => (0, -2, 0, -1, 0, 0, 0)\n4,1.5 => (1, 1, 1, 1, 1, 1, 0)\n\njulia> b = curve_class(G, Edge(1, 2))\n(0, 1)\n\njulia> Omega = get_Omega_beta(G, [b, 2*b, 3*b], 1; check_predictions = true, show_bar=false);\nCalculating b=(0, 1), g=0\nCalculating b=(0, 1), g=1\nCalculating b=(0, 2), g=0\nCalculating b=(0, 2), g=1\nCalculating b=(0, 3), g=0\nCalculating b=(0, 3), g=1\nPrediction holds for (0, 2)\nPrediction holds for (0, 3)\nPrediction fails for (0, 1):\n  prediction = 0\n\njulia> Omega[b]\n(1//12*t1^2*t2*u^2 + 1//12*t1^2*t4*u^2 + 1//12*t1^2*t6*u^2 + 1//12*t1*t2^2*u^2 + 1//6*t1*t2*t3*u^2 + 1//6*t1*t2*t5*u^2 - 1//6*t1*t2*t6*u^2 + 1//6*t1*t3*t4*u^2 + 1//6*t1*t3*t6*u^2 + 1//6*t1*t4*t5*u^2 + 1//6*t1*t5*t6*u^2 + 1//12*t1*t6^2*u^2 - t1 + 1//12*t2^2*t3*u^2 + 1//12*t2^2*t5*u^2 + 1//12*t2*t3^2*u^2 + 1//6*t2*t3*t5*u^2 - 1//6*t2*t3*t6*u^2 + 1//12*t2*t5^2*u^2 - 1//6*t2*t5*t6*u^2 + 1//12*t3^2*t4*u^2 + 1//12*t3^2*t6*u^2 + 1//6*t3*t4*t5*u^2 + 1//6*t3*t5*t6*u^2 + 1//12*t3*t6^2*u^2 - t3 + 1//12*t4*t5^2*u^2 + 1//12*t5^2*t6*u^2 + 1//12*t5*t6^2*u^2 - t5)//(t1*t2*t4*u^2 + 2*t1*t2*t6*u^2 + 1//2*t1*t4^2*u^2 + t1*t4*t6*u^2 + t2^2*t4*u^2 + 2*t2^2*t6*u^2 + t2*t3*t4*u^2 + 2*t2*t3*t6*u^2 + 3//2*t2*t4^2*u^2 + t2*t4*t5*u^2 + 4*t2*t4*t6*u^2 + 2*t2*t5*t6*u^2 + 2*t2*t6^2*u^2 + 1//2*t3*t4^2*u^2 + t3*t4*t6*u^2 + 1//2*t4^3*u^2 + 1//2*t4^2*t5*u^2 + 3//2*t4^2*t6*u^2 + t4*t5*t6*u^2 + t4*t6^2*u^2)\n\njulia> Omega[2*b]\n0\n\njulia> Omega[3*b]\n0\n\nAs we see, the conjectured value for Omega_beta is zero, but the true value is non-zero. The printed output highlights whenever the conjectured value is incorrect.\n\nMathematically, this example is not a counterexample to [HS26, Conjecture 5.1] because one of the conjecture's assumptions is not satisfied in this case.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GW_CY5.get_Omega_beta"},{"category":"function","location":"pipeline/#GW_CY5.get_GW_beta","page":"Pipeline","text":"get_GW_beta(G::AbstractGKM_graph, betas::Vector{T}, gMax::Int64; show_bar::Bool=true) where T <: CC\n\nLet (ZT) be a GKM space with GKM graph G. Return the truncated Grommov–Witten series\n\nsum_g ge 0^textgMaxu^2g-2 GW_gbeta(ZT)\n\nfor each curve class beta listed in beta.   The individual Gromov-Witten invariants are computed using the function gromov_witten   of GKMtools.\n\nThe output also contains the truncated Gromov-Witten series for each curve class with some positive integer multiple contained in betas.\n\nArguments\n\nG: the GKM graph of the GKM space (ZT) whose GW_beta(ZT) should be computed.\nbetas: the vector of curve classes on Z for which GW_beta(ZT) should be computed.\ngMax: the maximum genus up to which each GW_beta(ZT) will be approximated. That is,         the highest order term will be u^2textgMax-2.\nshow_bar: this optional argument can be set to false to disable the progress bar of the Gromov-Witten localization computation.\n\nExample\n\nLet us compute the Gromov-Witten series in degrees beta=123 for the example\n\nZ = textTot_mathbbP^1(mathcalO(-2))timesmathbbC^3\n\nand the T-action defined in [HS26, Example 1.1]. Its GKM graph can be obtained using gkm_graph_of_example_1_1.\n\nWe compute the Gromov-Witten series up to genus 1. That is, the powers of u that appear are u^-2 and 1.\n\njulia> G = gkm_graph_of_example_1_1();\n\njulia> beta = curve_class(G, Edge(1, 2));\n\njulia> gMax = 1;\n\njulia> GW = get_GW_beta(G, [beta, 2*beta, 3*beta], gMax; show_bar=false);\nCalculating b=(1), g=0\nCalculating b=(1), g=1\nCalculating b=(2), g=0\nCalculating b=(2), g=1\nCalculating b=(3), g=0\nCalculating b=(3), g=1\n\njulia> GW[beta]\n(1//12*t1*t4*t5*u^2 + 1//12*t1*t4*t6*u^2 + 1//12*t1*t5*t6*u^2 + t1 + 1//12*t2*t4*t5*u^2 + 1//12*t2*t4*t6*u^2 + 1//12*t2*t5*t6*u^2 + t2 + 1//12*t3*t4*t5*u^2 + 1//12*t3*t4*t6*u^2 + 1//12*t3*t5*t6*u^2 + t3)//(t4*t5*t6*u^2)\n\njulia> GW[2*beta]\n(1//24*t1*t4*t5*u^2 + 1//24*t1*t4*t6*u^2 + 1//24*t1*t5*t6*u^2 + 1//8*t1 + 1//24*t2*t4*t5*u^2 + 1//24*t2*t4*t6*u^2 + 1//24*t2*t5*t6*u^2 + 1//8*t2 + 1//24*t3*t4*t5*u^2 + 1//24*t3*t4*t6*u^2 + 1//24*t3*t5*t6*u^2 + 1//8*t3)//(t4*t5*t6*u^2)\n\njulia> GW[3*beta]\n(1//36*t1*t4*t5*u^2 + 1//36*t1*t4*t6*u^2 + 1//36*t1*t5*t6*u^2 + 1//27*t1 + 1//36*t2*t4*t5*u^2 + 1//36*t2*t4*t6*u^2 + 1//36*t2*t5*t6*u^2 + 1//27*t2 + 1//36*t3*t4*t5*u^2 + 1//36*t3*t4*t6*u^2 + 1//36*t3*t5*t6*u^2 + 1//27*t3)//(t4*t5*t6*u^2)\n\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GW_CY5.get_GW_beta"},{"category":"function","location":"pipeline/#GW_CY5.get_Omega_prediction","page":"Pipeline","text":"get_Omega_prediction(G::AbstractGKM_graph, t::Vector, u, beta, max_genus::Int64)\n\nReturn the conjectural prediction of Omega_beta for the space with GKM graph G.\n\nArguments\n\nG: the GKM graph of the space X whose Omega_beta we care about.\nt: the vector of equivariant parameters.\nu: the formal variable u that keeps track of the genus.\nbeta: the curve class beta for which the conjectural value of Omega_beta should be returned.\nmax_genus: the maximum genus up to which the conjectural value of Omega_beta should be returned.     That is, the highest exponent of u in the returned value is 2*max_genus - 1.\n\nnote: Note\nThis function requires the attribute :example_type of G to be set. This holds automatically whenever G is obtained from one of the constructors in the list of implemented spaces.\n\nExample\n\nLet us get the predicted Omega_beta for Z=mathcalA_2times mathbbC^3, continuing the example from get_Omega_beta.\n\njulia> G = Ar_times_C3(2);\n\njulia> b = curve_class(G, Edge(1, 2));\n\njulia> c = curve_class(G, Edge(2, 3));\n\njulia> max_genus = 1;\n\njulia> R, (t1, t2, t3, t4, t5, u) = polynomial_ring(QQ, [\"t1\", \"t2\", \"t3\", \"t4\", \"t5\", \"u\"])\n(Multivariate polynomial ring in 6 variables over QQ, QQMPolyRingElem[t1, t2, t3, t4, t5, u])\n\njulia> prediction = get_Omega_prediction(G, [t1, t2, t3, t4, t5], u, b, max_genus)\n(-1//12*t3^2*t4*u^2 - 1//12*t3^2*t5*u^2 - 1//12*t3*t4^2*u^2 - 1//4*t3*t4*t5*u^2 - 1//12*t3*t5^2*u^2 - t3 - 1//12*t4^2*t5*u^2 - 1//12*t4*t5^2*u^2 - t4 - t5)//(t3*t4*t5*u^2)\n\nThis does not yet match the result of get_Omega_beta above, because we still need to make the equivariantly Calabi-Yau substitution. The appropriate substitution is stored in G:\n\njulia> substitution = get_attribute(G, :equiCY_substitution)\n5-element Vector{QQMPolyRingElem}:\n t1\n t2\n -t2 - t4 - t5\n t4\n t5\n\njulia> # Translate the substitution vector to our own variables.\n        substitution = [evaluate(t, [t1, t2, t3, t4, t5]) for t in substitution]\n5-element Vector{QQMPolyRingElem}:\n t1\n t2\n -t2 - t4 - t5\n t4\n t5\n\njulia> evaluate(prediction, vcat(substitution, [u]))\n(1//12*t2^2*t4*u^2 + 1//12*t2^2*t5*u^2 + 1//12*t2*t4^2*u^2 + 1//12*t2*t4*t5*u^2 + 1//12*t2*t5^2*u^2 - t2)//(t2*t4*t5*u^2 + t4^2*t5*u^2 + t4*t5^2*u^2)\n\nThis matches the result of the example in get_Omega_beta, as required.\n\nThe above is an illustration of how the pipeline works. When get_Omega_beta is used with check_predictions=true, it uses the same mechanism to determine if the actual Omega_beta matches the conjectured one.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GW_CY5.get_Omega_prediction"},{"category":"section","location":"conj/P3_a_4a/#Evidence-4.8","page":"Local mathbbP^3 (Evidence 4.8)","text":"On this page, we present Julia code to reproduce the claims made in [HS26, Evidence 4.8] concerning the values of Omega_dL for\n\ntextTot(mathcalO_mathbbP^3(-a)oplusmathcalO_mathbbP^3(a-4))\n\nwith ain012. Under each line calling get_Omega_beta, we recorded the crucial line of the output as comment.","title":"Evidence 4.8"},{"category":"section","location":"conj/P3_a_4a/#\\mathcal{O}_{\\mathbb{P}3}(0)\\oplus-\\mathcal{O}_{\\mathbb{P}3}(-4)","page":"Local mathbbP^3 (Evidence 4.8)","text":"In this case (a=0), the GKM graph is obtained using gkm_5d_P3_04.\n\nusing Markdown\nMarkdown.parse(\"```julia\\n\" * read(joinpath(@__DIR__, \"..\", \"..\", \"..\", \"test\", \"results\", \"P3_04.jl\"), String) * \"\\n```\")","title":"mathcalO_mathbbP^3(0)oplus mathcalO_mathbbP^3(-4)"},{"category":"section","location":"conj/P3_a_4a/#\\mathcal{O}_{\\mathbb{P}3}(-1)\\oplus-\\mathcal{O}_{\\mathbb{P}3}(-3)","page":"Local mathbbP^3 (Evidence 4.8)","text":"In this case (a=1), the GKM graph is obtained using gkm_5d_P3_13.\n\nusing Markdown\nMarkdown.parse(\"```julia\\n\" * read(joinpath(@__DIR__, \"..\", \"..\", \"..\", \"test\", \"results\", \"P3_13.jl\"), String) * \"\\n```\")","title":"mathcalO_mathbbP^3(-1)oplus mathcalO_mathbbP^3(-3)"},{"category":"section","location":"conj/P3_a_4a/#\\mathcal{O}_{\\mathbb{P}3}(-2){\\oplus-2}","page":"Local mathbbP^3 (Evidence 4.8)","text":"In this case (a=2), the GKM graph is obtained using gkm_5d_P3_22.\n\nusing Markdown\nMarkdown.parse(\"```julia\\n\" * read(joinpath(@__DIR__, \"..\", \"..\", \"..\", \"test\", \"results\", \"P3_22.jl\"), String) * \"\\n```\")","title":"mathcalO_mathbbP^3(-2)^oplus 2"},{"category":"section","location":"conj/closed_vertex/#Evidence-2.13","page":"Closed vertex (Evidence 2.13)","text":"We provide code to reproduce our evidence on [HS26, Conjecture 2.11] which concerns T-spaces of the form XtimesmathbbC^2 where X is the 3d closed vertex.\n\nThe following Julia code verifies the conjecture [HS26, Conjecture 2.11] up to genus 3 in the classes stated in the paper. In the code, the curve classes b1,b2,b3 correspond to Q_1Q_2Q_3 in the paper.\n\nUnder each line calling get_Omega_beta, we recorded the crucial line of the output as a comment.\n\nusing Markdown\nMarkdown.parse(\"```julia\\n\" * read(joinpath(@__DIR__, \"..\", \"..\", \"..\", \"test\", \"results\", \"cl_vertex.jl\"), String) * \"\\n```\")","title":"Evidence 2.13"},{"category":"section","location":"pt/pt_conj/overview/#Overview-of-PT-evidence","page":"Overview of PT evidence","text":"In this section, we explain how our PT calculations cited as Evidence in [HS26] can be reproduced.\n\nSection/Space Evidence Code\nClosed vertex Evidence 2.12 closedvertex.sage & closedvertex.ipynb\nLocal mathbbP^2 Evidence 4.4 & 5.7 local_P2.sage\nLocal mathbbP^3 Evidence 4.9 local_P3.sage\nLocal mathbbP^1 times mathbbP^1 Evidence 5.7 local_P1xP1.sage\n\nnote: Note\nNote that we executed most of the code listed in this section on a compute cluster provided by the Mathematics Department of ETH Zürich with the following metrics.Operating System: Fedora\nCPU: 1x AMD EPYC 7763 (64 Cores) 2.45GHz\nRAM: 512 GBTherefore, it may be hard to reproduce some of our examples on an ordinary PC.","title":"Overview of PT evidence"},{"category":"section","location":"conj/gw_gauge/#Evidence-3.5","page":"Gauge theory (Evidence 3.5)","text":"On this page, we provide Julia code to verify [HS26, Conjecture 3.3] concerning the T-space X_NmtimesmathbbC^2 obtained from gkm_5d_gauge. The curve classes F[1],...,F[N-1] and B[1],...,B[N] in the code correspond to F_1dotsF_N-1 and C_1dotsC_N in the paper, respectively, see [HS26, Figure 7].\n\nUnder each line calling get_Omega_beta, we recorded the crucial line of the output as a comment.","title":"Evidence 3.5"},{"category":"section","location":"conj/gw_gauge/#N2,-m-1","page":"Gauge theory (Evidence 3.5)","text":"using Markdown\nMarkdown.parse(\"```julia\\n\" * read(joinpath(@__DIR__, \"..\", \"..\", \"..\", \"test\", \"results\", \"gauge\", \"gauge_N2_m1.jl\"), String) * \"\\n```\")","title":"N=2 m=-1"},{"category":"section","location":"conj/gw_gauge/#N2,-m0","page":"Gauge theory (Evidence 3.5)","text":"using Markdown\nMarkdown.parse(\"```julia\\n\" * read(joinpath(@__DIR__, \"..\", \"..\", \"..\", \"test\", \"results\", \"gauge\", \"gauge_N2_0.jl\"), String) * \"\\n```\")","title":"N=2 m=0"},{"category":"section","location":"conj/gw_gauge/#N2,-m1","page":"Gauge theory (Evidence 3.5)","text":"using Markdown\nMarkdown.parse(\"```julia\\n\" * read(joinpath(@__DIR__, \"..\", \"..\", \"..\", \"test\", \"results\", \"gauge\", \"gauge_N2_1.jl\"), String) * \"\\n```\")","title":"N=2 m=1"},{"category":"section","location":"conj/gw_gauge/#N3,-m-2","page":"Gauge theory (Evidence 3.5)","text":"using Markdown\nMarkdown.parse(\"```julia\\n\" * read(joinpath(@__DIR__, \"..\", \"..\", \"..\", \"test\", \"results\", \"gauge\", \"gauge_N3_m2.jl\"), String) * \"\\n```\")","title":"N=3 m=-2"},{"category":"section","location":"conj/gw_gauge/#N3,-m-1","page":"Gauge theory (Evidence 3.5)","text":"using Markdown\nMarkdown.parse(\"```julia\\n\" * read(joinpath(@__DIR__, \"..\", \"..\", \"..\", \"test\", \"results\", \"gauge\", \"gauge_N3_m1.jl\"), String) * \"\\n```\")","title":"N=3 m=-1"},{"category":"section","location":"conj/gw_gauge/#N3,-m0","page":"Gauge theory (Evidence 3.5)","text":"using Markdown\nMarkdown.parse(\"```julia\\n\" * read(joinpath(@__DIR__, \"..\", \"..\", \"..\", \"test\", \"results\", \"gauge\", \"gauge_N3_0.jl\"), String) * \"\\n```\")","title":"N=3 m=0"},{"category":"section","location":"conj/gw_gauge/#N3,-m1","page":"Gauge theory (Evidence 3.5)","text":"using Markdown\nMarkdown.parse(\"```julia\\n\" * read(joinpath(@__DIR__, \"..\", \"..\", \"..\", \"test\", \"results\", \"gauge\", \"gauge_N3_1.jl\"), String) * \"\\n```\")","title":"N=3 m=1"},{"category":"section","location":"conj/gw_gauge/#N3,-m2","page":"Gauge theory (Evidence 3.5)","text":"using Markdown\nMarkdown.parse(\"```julia\\n\" * read(joinpath(@__DIR__, \"..\", \"..\", \"..\", \"test\", \"results\", \"gauge\", \"gauge_N3_2.jl\"), String) * \"\\n```\")","title":"N=3 m=2"},{"category":"section","location":"conj/gw_gauge/#Necessity-of-m-N","page":"Gauge theory (Evidence 3.5)","text":"Let N=3 and m=4 so that mge N. In this example, all equivariant parameters t1, t3, t4, t5 appear in Omega_beta, which shows that mN is in general a necessary assumption for [HS26, Conjecture 3.3] to hold. Indeed, for mN, the conjectured formula only contains the equivariant parameters t4 and t5. (Note that t2 does not appear because it is expressed in terms of the other parameters on the equivariantly Calabi-Yau torus.)\n\nUnder each call to get_Omega_beta, we include the output of the computed values for Omega_beta as a comment, showing the equivariant parameters t1, t3, t4, t5.\n\nusing Markdown\nMarkdown.parse(\"```julia\\n\" * read(joinpath(@__DIR__, \"..\", \"..\", \"..\", \"test\", \"results\", \"gauge\", \"gauge_N3_m4.jl\"), String) * \"\\n```\")","title":"Necessity of m  N"},{"category":"section","location":"pt/pt_conj/closed_vertex/#Closed-vertex","page":"Closed vertex (Evidence 2.12)","text":"The so-called closed vertex X is discussed in [HS26, Section 2.4]. A closed formula for the PT series of X is presented in Conjecture 2.11. Evidence 2.12 for this conjecture can be reproduced by running the script closed_vertex.sage. For convenience, we also included a Jupyter notebook closed_vertex.ipynb containing multiple comments on our code.\n\nThe script will generate the following output:\n\nExpansion of the plethystic exponential relating membrane indices and the PT series took 40.38h.\nNow we check the formulae against PT series in multi-degree (d1, d2, d3) with d1 + d2 + d3 <= 4 for 6 orders beyond the leading order in q.\n\n[d1, d2, d3] = [1, 0, 0]  -->  True  (0.00h)\n[d1, d2, d3] = [2, 0, 0]  -->  True  (0.02h)\n[d1, d2, d3] = [1, 1, 0]  -->  True  (0.00h)\n[d1, d2, d3] = [3, 0, 0]  -->  True  (1.07h)\n[d1, d2, d3] = [2, 1, 0]  -->  True  (0.61h)\n[d1, d2, d3] = [1, 1, 1]  -->  True  (3.54h)\n[d1, d2, d3] = [4, 0, 0]  -->  True  (17.81h)\n[d1, d2, d3] = [3, 1, 0]  -->  True  (41.58h)\n[d1, d2, d3] = [2, 2, 0]  -->  True  (30.21h)\n[d1, d2, d3] = [2, 1, 1]  -->  True  (64.56h)\n\nEach line corresponds to a check of the formula for mathsfPT_(d_1d_2d_3)(ZmathbbC^times_qmathsfT) where Z = X times mathbbC^2. If the conjectured formula holds true we print True. In brackets we provide information on how long each check took.","title":"Closed vertex"},{"category":"section","location":"pt/pt_conj/local_P3/#Local-\\mathbb{P}3","page":"Local mathbbP^3 (Evidence 4.9)","text":"The purpose of the file local_P3.sage is to test our conjectural formulas for membrane indices of the fivefolds\n\nmathrmTot_mathbbP^3 mathcalO(-a) oplus mathcalO(a-4) qquad 0 leq a leq 4\n\nagainst PT calculations. The formulae for membrane indices are partly stated in [HS26, Section 4.2]. All remaining formulae can be found in CY5\\tests\\omega_data\\.\n\nThe script local_P2.sage reads the files with our conjectural formulae and compares them with the PT series computed via the K-theoretic vertex. The script produces the following output, which we record in our paper as [HS26, Evidence 4.9]:\n\n--------------------\nTot_P3 O(0) + O(-4)\n--------------------\nFinished reading membrane indices of Tot_P3 O(0) + O(-4). This took 0.01h.\nNow we compute the plethystic exponential up to degree 3.\n\nExpansion of conjectural formula took 0.04h.\nNow we check the formula up to 7 orders beyond leading order in q.\n\nTot_P3 O(0) + O(-4), d = 1  -->  True  (0.00h)\nTot_P3 O(0) + O(-4), d = 2  -->  True  (0.57h)\nTot_P3 O(0) + O(-4), d = 3  -->  True  (136.98h)\n\n--------------------\nTot_P3 O(-1) + O(-3)\n--------------------\nFinished reading membrane indices of Tot_P3 O(-1) + O(-3). This took 0.06h.\nNow we compute the plethystic exponential up to degree 2.\n\nExpansion of conjectural formula took 0.97h.\nNow we check the formula up to 7 orders beyond leading order in q.\n\nTot_P3 O(-1) + O(-3), d = 1  -->  True  (0.00h)\nTot_P3 O(-1) + O(-3), d = 2  -->  True  (0.69h)\n\n--------------------\nTot_P3 O(-2) + O(-2)\n--------------------\nFinished reading membrane indices of Tot_P3 O(-2) + O(-2). This took 0.00h.\nNow we compute the plethystic exponential up to degree 3.\n\nExpansion of conjectural formula took 0.00h.\nNow we check the formula up to 7 orders beyond leading order in q.\n\nTot_P3 O(-2) + O(-2), d = 1  -->  True  (0.00h)\nTot_P3 O(-2) + O(-2), d = 2  -->  True  (0.31h)\nTot_P3 O(-2) + O(-2), d = 3  -->  True  (48.94h)\n\n--------------------\nTot_P3 O(-3) + O(-1)\n--------------------\nFinished reading membrane indices of Tot_P3 O(-3) + O(-1). This took 0.23h.\nNow we compute the plethystic exponential up to degree 2.\n\nExpansion of conjectural formula took 0.97h.\nNow we check the formula up to 7 orders beyond leading order in q.\n\nTot_P3 O(-3) + O(-1), d = 1  -->  True  (0.00h)\nTot_P3 O(-3) + O(-1), d = 2  -->  True  (0.68h)\n\n--------------------\nTot_P3 O(-4) + O(0)\n--------------------\nFinished reading membrane indices of Tot_P3 O(-4) + O(0). This took 0.01h.\nNow we compute the plethystic exponential up to degree 3.\n\nExpansion of conjectural formula took 0.06h.\nNow we check the formula up to 7 orders beyond leading order in q.\n\nTot_P3 O(-4) + O(0), d = 1  -->  True  (0.00h)\nTot_P3 O(-4) + O(0), d = 2  -->  True  (0.44h)\nTot_P3 O(-4) + O(0), d = 3  -->  True  (50.58h)","title":"Local mathbbP^3"},{"category":"section","location":"pt/pt_conj/local_P2/#Local-\\mathbb{P}2","page":"Local mathbbP^2 (Evidence 4.4 & 5.7)","text":"The purpose of the file local_P2.sage is to test different claims made in [HS26] about PT series of threefolds inside local projective planes.\n\nMore precisely, for the fivefold\n\nmathrmTot_mathbbP^2 mathcalO(-1) oplus mathcalO(-1) oplus mathcalO(-1)\n\nwe show that the PT series of the threefold mathrmTot_mathbbP^2 mathcalO(-1) inside this ambient space matches with some explicit formulae for membrane indices Omega_dH in degree d leq 4 as discussed in [HS26, Section 4.1].\n\nFor the fivefold\n\nZ=mathrmTot_mathbbP^2 mathcalO(-2) oplus mathcalO(-1) oplus mathcalO\n\nwe test that for all six distinct choices of fibrewise actions of mathbbC^times_q on Z with three-dimensional fixed locus X, the associated PT series vanishes. This is a special instance of [HS26, Conjecture 5.6].\n\nBy running the script local_P2.sage one can hence reproduce Evidence 4.4 and parts of Evidence 5.7 in our paper. The other part of Evidence 5.7 is checked here. The script produces the following output:\n\n----------------------------\nTot_P2 O(-1) + O(-1) + O(-1)\n----------------------------\nFinished reading Omega. This took 5.29h.\nNow we compute the plethystic exponential\nExpansion of conjectural formula took 25.64h.\nNow we check the formula up to 7 orders beyond leading order in q and degree d <= 4.\n\nTot_P2 O(-1) + O(-1) + O(-1), d = 1  -->  True  (0.00h)\nTot_P2 O(-1) + O(-1) + O(-1), d = 2  -->  True  (0.09h)\nTot_P2 O(-1) + O(-1) + O(-1), d = 3  -->  True  (16.93h)\nTot_P2 O(-1) + O(-1) + O(-1), d = 4  -->  True  (932.29h)\n\n---------------------------------------------\nTot_P2 O(-2)  in  Tot_P2 O(-2) + O(-1) + O(0)\n---------------------------------------------\nWe test the vanishing of the PT series up to 6 orders beyond leading order in q and degree d <= 4:\nTot_P2 O(-2) + O(-1) + O(0), d = 1  -->  True  (0.29h)\nTot_P2 O(-2) + O(-1) + O(0), d = 2  -->  True  (8.08h)\nTot_P2 O(-2) + O(-1) + O(0), d = 3  -->  True  (439.06h)\nTot_P2 O(-2) + O(-1) + O(0), d = 4  -->  True  (11595.40h)\n\n---------------------------------------------\nTot_P2 O(-2)  in  Tot_P2 O(-2) + O(0) + O(-1)\n---------------------------------------------\nWe test the vanishing of the PT series up to 6 orders beyond leading order in q and degree d <= 4:\nTot_P2 O(-2) + O(0) + O(-1), d = 1  -->  True  (0.07h)\nTot_P2 O(-2) + O(0) + O(-1), d = 2  -->  True  (7.76h)\nTot_P2 O(-2) + O(0) + O(-1), d = 3  -->  True  (417.85h)\nTot_P2 O(-2) + O(0) + O(-1), d = 4  -->  True  (11926.15h)\n\n---------------------------------------------\nTot_P2 O(-1)  in  Tot_P2 O(-1) + O(-2) + O(0)\n---------------------------------------------\nWe test the vanishing of the PT series up to 6 orders beyond leading order in q and degree d <= 4:\nTot_P2 O(-1) + O(-2) + O(0), d = 1  -->  True  (0.06h)\nTot_P2 O(-1) + O(-2) + O(0), d = 2  -->  True  (7.32h)\nTot_P2 O(-1) + O(-2) + O(0), d = 3  -->  True  (385.44h)\nTot_P2 O(-1) + O(-2) + O(0), d = 4  -->  True  (10256.00h)\n\n---------------------------------------------\nTot_P2 O(-1)  in  Tot_P2 O(-1) + O(0) + O(-2)\n---------------------------------------------\nWe test the vanishing of the PT series up to 6 orders beyond leading order in q and degree d <= 4:\nTot_P2 O(-1) + O(0) + O(-2), d = 1  -->  True  (0.06h)\nTot_P2 O(-1) + O(0) + O(-2), d = 2  -->  True  (7.69h)\nTot_P2 O(-1) + O(0) + O(-2), d = 3  -->  True  (393.64h)\nTot_P2 O(-1) + O(0) + O(-2), d = 4  -->  True  (10017.49h)\n\n---------------------------------------------\nTot_P2 O(0)  in  Tot_P2 O(0) + O(-2) + O(-1)\n---------------------------------------------\nWe test the vanishing of the PT series up to 6 orders beyond leading order in q and degree d <= 4:\nTot_P2 O(0) + O(-2) + O(-1), d = 1  -->  True  (0.06h)\nTot_P2 O(0) + O(-2) + O(-1), d = 2  -->  True  (9.24h)\nTot_P2 O(0) + O(-2) + O(-1), d = 3  -->  True  (405.34h)\nTot_P2 O(0) + O(-2) + O(-1), d = 4  -->  True  (9712.55h)\n\n---------------------------------------------\nTot_P2 O(0)  in  Tot_P2 O(0) + O(-1) + O(-2)\n---------------------------------------------\nWe test the vanishing of the PT series up to 6 orders beyond leading order in q and degree d <= 4:\nTot_P2 O(0) + O(-1) + O(-2), d = 1  -->  True  (0.06h)\nTot_P2 O(0) + O(-1) + O(-2), d = 2  -->  True  (8.76h)\nTot_P2 O(0) + O(-1) + O(-2), d = 3  -->  True  (419.47h)\nTot_P2 O(0) + O(-1) + O(-2), d = 4  -->  True  (9979.44h)\n","title":"Local mathbbP^2"},{"category":"section","location":"conj/P2_111/#Evidence-4.2","page":"Local mathbbP^2 (Evidence 4.2)","text":"On this page, we provide Julia code to verify that the formula for Omega_dH on textTot(mathcalO_mathbbP^2(-1)^oplus 3) given by [HS26, equation (29)] matches the values computed from Gromov-Witten theory.\n\nThe GKM graph is obtained using gkm_5d_P2_111. The curve classes b, 2*b, 3*b, 4*b in the code below correspond to H 2H 3H 4H in [HS26, Table 3]. Under each line calling get_Omega_beta, we recorded the crucial line of the output as a comment.\n\nusing Markdown\nMarkdown.parse(\"```julia\\n\" * read(joinpath(@__DIR__, \"..\", \"..\", \"..\", \"test\", \"results\", \"P2_111.jl\"), String) * \"\\n```\")","title":"Evidence 4.2"},{"category":"section","location":"#Welcome!","page":"Home","text":"This website accompanies the paper Experiments with Membranes, Maps and Sheaves by Yannik Schuler and Daniel Holmes [HS26]. It consists of two parts:\n\nComputations in Gromov-Witten theory using GW_CY5.jl, a Julia package to compute the membrane indices Omega_beta of toric CY5s and check the conjectural formulas in [HS26]. It builds on GKMtools.jl [HM25a, HM25b] to compute equivariant Gromov-Witten invariants of the relevant spaces.\nComputations in Pandharipande-Thomas theory using PT_CY5.sage, leading to the conjectural formulas in [HS26]. This builds on [Liu25].","title":"Welcome!"},{"category":"section","location":"pt/pt_conj/local_P1xP1/#Local-\\mathbb{P}1-\\times-\\mathbb{P}1","page":"Local mathbbP^1 times mathbbP^1 (Evidence 5.7)","text":"The purpose of the file local_P1xP1.sage is to probe the vanishing claimed for the two local surfaces\n\nmathrmTot_mathbbP^1 times mathbbP^1 mathcalO(-1-1) oplus mathcalO(-1-1) oplus mathcalO\n\nand\n\nmathrmTot_mathbbP^1 times mathbbP^1 mathcalO(-20) oplus mathcalO(0-2) oplus mathcalO\n\nin [HS26, Evidence 5.7].\n\nFor the first, the claim is that the PT series of any threefold inside the fivefold vanishes. We test this for the two distinct choices of threefolds that arise as some fixed locus of a fibre-wise mathbbC^times_q-action on the fivefold.\n\nFor the second local surface the claim is that all membrane indices but Omega_(10) and Omega_(01) vanish. For the two non-zero indices we also provide a conjectural formula. We check the formula for two choices of a threefold in the fivefold.\n\nThe script local_P1xP1.sage can be used to probe this claim. It creates the following output:\n\nThe PT invariants of the following targets all cojecturally vanish.\nWe test this conjecture up to 6 orders beyond leading order in q in q and bi-degrees (d1,d2) with d1 + d2 <= 4:\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------\nTot_P1xP1 O(-1,-1)  in  Tot_P1xP1 O(-1,-1) + O(-1,-1) + O(0,0)\n--------------------------------------------------------------\n(d1,d2) = (0,0)  -->  True  (0.00h)\n(d1,d2) = (0,1)  -->  True  (0.00h)\n(d1,d2) = (0,2)  -->  True  (0.01h)\n(d1,d2) = (0,3)  -->  True  (0.72h)\n(d1,d2) = (0,4)  -->  True  (18.85h)\n(d1,d2) = (1,0)  -->  True  (0.00h)\n(d1,d2) = (1,1)  -->  True  (0.00h)\n(d1,d2) = (1,2)  -->  True  (1.13h)\n(d1,d2) = (1,3)  -->  True  (34.30h)\n(d1,d2) = (2,0)  -->  True  (0.01h)\n(d1,d2) = (2,1)  -->  True  (0.13h)\n(d1,d2) = (2,2)  -->  True  (17.92h)\n(d1,d2) = (3,0)  -->  True  (0.17h)\n(d1,d2) = (3,1)  -->  True  (1.75h)\n(d1,d2) = (4,0)  -->  True  (2.40h)\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------\nTot_P1xP1 O(0,0)  in  Tot_P1xP1 O(0,0) + O(-1,-1) + O(-1,-1)\n--------------------------------------------------------------\n(d1,d2) = (0,0)  -->  True  (0.00h)\n(d1,d2) = (0,1)  -->  True  (0.00h)\n(d1,d2) = (0,2)  -->  True  (0.01h)\n(d1,d2) = (0,3)  -->  True  (0.17h)\n(d1,d2) = (0,4)  -->  True  (2.51h)\n(d1,d2) = (1,0)  -->  True  (0.00h)\n(d1,d2) = (1,1)  -->  True  (0.00h)\n(d1,d2) = (1,2)  -->  True  (0.15h)\n(d1,d2) = (1,3)  -->  True  (1.68h)\n(d1,d2) = (2,0)  -->  True  (0.01h)\n(d1,d2) = (2,1)  -->  True  (0.15h)\n(d1,d2) = (2,2)  -->  True  (3.04h)\n(d1,d2) = (3,0)  -->  True  (0.17h)\n(d1,d2) = (3,1)  -->  True  (1.81h)\n(d1,d2) = (4,0)  -->  True  (2.47h)\n\nStart setting up the conjectural formulas for the next PT series to analyse...\nDone! This took 11.59h.\nNow we check the formula up to 6 orders beyond leading order in q and bi-degrees (d1,d2) with d1 + d2 <= 4:\n\n-----------------------------------------------------------\nTot_P1xP1 O(-2,0)  in  Tot_P1xP1 O(-2,0) + O(0,-2) + O(0,0)\n-----------------------------------------------------------\n(d1,d2) = (0,0)  -->  True  (0.00h)\n(d1,d2) = (0,1)  -->  True  (0.00h)\n(d1,d2) = (0,2)  -->  True  (0.01h)\n(d1,d2) = (0,3)  -->  True  (0.18h)\n(d1,d2) = (0,4)  -->  True  (2.75h)\n(d1,d2) = (1,0)  -->  True  (0.00h)\n(d1,d2) = (1,1)  -->  True  (0.00h)\n(d1,d2) = (1,2)  -->  True  (0.15h)\n(d1,d2) = (1,3)  -->  True  (1.78h)\n(d1,d2) = (2,0)  -->  True  (0.01h)\n(d1,d2) = (2,1)  -->  True  (0.15h)\n(d1,d2) = (2,2)  -->  True  (3.06h)\n(d1,d2) = (3,0)  -->  True  (0.44h)\n(d1,d2) = (3,1)  -->  True  (1.68h)\n(d1,d2) = (4,0)  -->  True  (2.23h)\n\n-----------------------------------------------------------\nTot_P1xP1 O(0,0)  in  Tot_P1xP1 O(0,0) + O(0,-2) + O(-2,0)\n-----------------------------------------------------------\n(d1,d2) = (0,0)  -->  True  (0.00h)\n(d1,d2) = (0,1)  -->  True  (0.00h)\n(d1,d2) = (0,2)  -->  True  (0.01h)\n(d1,d2) = (0,3)  -->  True  (0.53h)\n(d1,d2) = (0,4)  -->  True  (8.57h)\n(d1,d2) = (1,0)  -->  True  (0.00h)\n(d1,d2) = (1,1)  -->  True  (0.00h)\n(d1,d2) = (1,2)  -->  True  (0.14h)\n(d1,d2) = (1,3)  -->  True  (1.93h)\n(d1,d2) = (2,0)  -->  True  (0.01h)\n(d1,d2) = (2,1)  -->  True  (0.15h)\n(d1,d2) = (2,2)  -->  True  (3.65h)\n(d1,d2) = (3,0)  -->  True  (0.21h)\n(d1,d2) = (3,1)  -->  True  (2.04h)\n(d1,d2) = (4,0)  -->  True  (12.37h)\n\nThese checks confirm a part of [HS26, Evidence 5.7]. For the other part see here.","title":"Local mathbbP^1 times mathbbP^1"},{"category":"section","location":"example/#Details-of-the-Pipeline","page":"Details of the Pipeline","text":"On this page, we present a detailed example illustrating the Pipeline and how it works internally. The main function get_Omega_beta and its two most important helpers get_GW_beta and get_Omega_prediction have already been introduced on the page Pipeline. We will therefore focus on the internal features that make these functions work.\n\nThroughout, we assume that the user has already loaded all required packages using the following line.\n\njulia> using Oscar, GKMtools, GW_CY5","title":"Details of the Pipeline"},{"category":"section","location":"example/#Loading-the-space","page":"Details of the Pipeline","text":"Throughout, we will be dealing with the Calabi-Yau 5-fold\n\nZ = mathcalA_2timesmathbbC^2\n\nand its equivariantly Calabi-Yau T-action given by its description as a strip geometry, see [HS26, Section 2.1]. Let G be the GKM graph of (ZT) and let betain H_2(Z) be the curve class associated to the leftmost edge in [HS26, picture (18)]. We load G using the function Ar_times_C3.\n\njulia> G = Ar_times_C3(2)\nGKM graph with 3 nodes, valency 5 and axial function:\n2,1 -> 1,1 => (1, -1, 0, 0, 0)\n3,1 -> 2,1 => (1, -2, 0, 0, 0)\nStandalone flags:\n1,1.2 => (0, -1, 0, -1, -1)\n1,1.3 => (1, 0, 0, 0, 0)\n1,1.4 => (0, 0, 0, 1, 0)\n1,1.5 => (0, 0, 0, 0, 1)\n2,1.3 => (0, -1, 0, -1, -1)\n2,1.4 => (0, 0, 0, 1, 0)\n2,1.5 => (0, 0, 0, 0, 1)\n3,1.2 => (0, -1, 0, -1, -1)\n3,1.3 => (-1, 3, 0, 0, 0)\n3,1.4 => (0, 0, 0, 1, 0)\n3,1.5 => (0, 0, 0, 0, 1)\n\nWe also load beta.\n\njulia> beta = curve_class(G, Edge(1, 2))\n(1, 0)","title":"Loading the space"},{"category":"section","location":"example/#Setting-conjectures-for-\\Omega_\\beta","page":"Details of the Pipeline","text":"Let us see what the predictions for Omega_beta and Omega_2beta are up to genus one. As discussed in Pipeline, this is given to us by the function get_Omega_prediction as follows.\n\njulia> R, (t1, t2, t3, t4, t5, u) = polynomial_ring(QQ, [\"t1\", \"t2\", \"t3\", \"t4\", \"t5\", \"u\"]);\n\njulia> max_genus = 1;\n\njulia> get_Omega_prediction(G, [t1, t2, t3, t4, t5], u, beta, max_genus)\n(-1//12*t3^2*t4*u^2 - 1//12*t3^2*t5*u^2 - 1//12*t3*t4^2*u^2 - 1//4*t3*t4*t5*u^2 - 1//12*t3*t5^2*u^2 - t3 - 1//12*t4^2*t5*u^2 - 1//12*t4*t5^2*u^2 - t4 - t5)//(t3*t4*t5*u^2)\n\njulia> get_Omega_prediction(G, [t1, t2, t3, t4, t5], u, 2*beta, max_genus)\n0","title":"Setting conjectures for Omega_beta"},{"category":"section","location":"example/#Where-does-this-come-from?","page":"Details of the Pipeline","text":"Internally, get_Omega_prediction does the following.\n\njulia> get_attribute(G, :example_type)\n:gkm_5d_strip_from_3d_CY\n\nThis tells the function that our space G is of the type strip geometry. It then calls the internal function GW_CY5.gkm_5d_strip_prediction which produces the specific expression of Omega_beta that is conjectured to hold for strip geometries:\n\njulia> GW_CY5.gkm_5d_strip_prediction(G, [t1, t2, t3, t4, t5], u, beta, max_genus)\n(-1//12*t3^2*t4*u^2 - 1//12*t3^2*t5*u^2 - 1//12*t3*t4^2*u^2 - 1//4*t3*t4*t5*u^2 - 1//12*t3*t5^2*u^2 - t3 - 1//12*t4^2*t5*u^2 - 1//12*t4*t5^2*u^2 - t4 - t5)//(t3*t4*t5*u^2)\n\njulia> GW_CY5.gkm_5d_strip_prediction(G, [t1, t2, t3, t4, t5], u, 2*beta, max_genus)\n0\n\nSimilarly, all other spaces listed in Spaces have the attribute :example_type. For each example type, we have implemented a dedicated prediction function that is called by get_Omega_prediction, based on this attribute.","title":"Where does this come from?"},{"category":"section","location":"example/#General-vs.-manual-prediction-functions","page":"Details of the Pipeline","text":"Most conjectured formulae for Omega_beta have a sufficiently compact form that we could implement directly so that the degree is an arbitrary parameter (\"general prediction\"). This applies to all spaces except those listed in Further Examples. For those, the only way to arrive at the conjectural expression of Omega_beta was by degree-wise computations in PT theory, where the size of the output grows rapidly as the degree increases. For these examples, we saved the resulting expressions in the folder test/omega_data/ (\"manual prediction\"). These expressions are then loaded into Julia by the internal predictor function of the example in question.\n\nFor example, the internal prediction function for the space textTot_mathbbP^2(mathcalO(-1)oplusmathcalO(-1)oplusmathcalO(-1)) given by gkm_5d_P2_111 is GW_CY5.gkm_5d_P2_111_prediction. It reads the files test/omega_data/Omega_P2_111_num.dat and test/omega_data/Omega_P2_111_den.dat to produce the following output in degree one and genus up to 2.\n\njulia> Tot_P2_111 = gkm_5d_P2_111();\n\njulia> get_attribute(Tot_P2_111, :example_type)\n:P2_111\n\njulia> b = curve_class(Tot_P2_111, Edge(1, 2));\n\njulia> GW_CY5.gkm_5d_P2_111_prediction(Tot_P2_111, [t1, t2, t3, t4, t5], u, b, 2)\n1//32*t1^2*u^2 - 1//32*t1*t2*u^2 - 1//32*t1*t3*u^2 + 1//32*t2^2*u^2 - 1//32*t2*t3*u^2 + 1//32*t3^2*u^2 - 1//8\n\nIndeed, this matches the output of get_Omega_prediction:\n\njulia> get_Omega_prediction(Tot_P2_111, [t1, t2, t3, t4, t5], u, b, 2)\n1//32*t1^2*u^2 - 1//32*t1*t2*u^2 - 1//32*t1*t3*u^2 + 1//32*t2^2*u^2 - 1//32*t2*t3*u^2 + 1//32*t3^2*u^2 - 1//8\n\nWe now return to the running example Z = mathcalA_2timesmathbbC^2 of this page.","title":"General vs. manual prediction functions"},{"category":"section","location":"example/#Getting-\\Omega_\\beta-from-GW-invariants","page":"Details of the Pipeline","text":"Next, we explain how get_Omega_beta computes the membrane indices Omega_beta from the truncated Gromov-Witten generating series produced by get_GW_beta. In our running example Z = mathcalA_2timesmathbbC^2, this produces the following in degree up to 2 and maximum genus 1.\n\njulia> GW = get_GW_beta(G, [beta, 2*beta], max_genus; show_bar=false);\nCalculating b=(1, 0), g=0\nCalculating b=(1, 0), g=1\nCalculating b=(2, 0), g=0\nCalculating b=(2, 0), g=1\n\njulia> GW[beta]\n(1//12*t2^2*t4*u^2 + 1//12*t2^2*t5*u^2 + 1//12*t2*t4^2*u^2 + 1//12*t2*t4*t5*u^2 + 1//12*t2*t5^2*u^2 - t2)//(t2*t4*t5*u^2 + t4^2*t5*u^2 + t4*t5^2*u^2)\n\njulia> GW[2*beta]\n(1//24*t2^2*t4*u^2 + 1//24*t2^2*t5*u^2 + 1//24*t2*t4^2*u^2 + 1//24*t2*t4*t5*u^2 + 1//24*t2*t5^2*u^2 - 1//8*t2)//(t2*t4*t5*u^2 + t4^2*t5*u^2 + t4*t5^2*u^2)\n\nRecall the defining formula for Omega_beta:\n\nsum_betaneq 0sum_gge 0 Q^beta u^2g-2  GW_gbeta(XT)\n=\nsum_betaneq 0\nsum_k0 frac1k Q^kbeta Omega_beta(epsilonku)\n\nExtracting the coefficient of Q^beta gives\n\nsum_gge 0 u^2g-2GW_gbeta(XT)\n=\nsum_kmid beta \nfrac1k Omega_betak(epsilonku)\n\nOn the right, the sum is over all positive integers k dividing beta. Therefore, Omega_beta can be computed if we know\n\nthe left hand side,\nand Omega_betak for all k 1 dividing beta.\n\nThus, we can compute Omega_beta recursively, which is implemented as follows:","title":"Getting Omega_beta from GW invariants"},{"category":"section","location":"example/#Determining-all-relevant-curve-classes","page":"Details of the Pipeline","text":"If we want to compute Omega_beta for all beta in some set S of curve classes, the above recursion requires us to compute Omega_beta and sum_g=0^textgMax u^2g-2GW_gbeta(XT) for all beta that have some positive integer multiple in S.\n\nThe internal function GW_CY5.downward_close_cc takes a list S of curve classes and returns all curve classes with some positive integer multiple contained in S:\n\njulia> GW_CY5.downward_close_ccs([6*beta, 10*beta])\n6-element Vector{AbstractAlgebra.Generic.FreeModuleElem{ZZRingElem}}:\n (6, 0)\n (3, 0)\n (2, 0)\n (1, 0)\n (10, 0)\n (5, 0)\n\nIndeed, for S=6beta 10beta, the set of curve classes with positive integer multiple in S is beta 2beta 3beta 5beta 6beta 10beta. The function get_Omega_beta calls get_GW_beta, which in turn uses GW_CY5.downward_close_cc to determine all relevant curve classes.","title":"Determining all relevant curve classes"},{"category":"section","location":"example/#Solving-the-recursion-for-\\Omega_\\beta","page":"Details of the Pipeline","text":"Once get_GW_beta returned the truncated Gromov-Witten series in all relevant curve classes, get_Omega_beta proceeds by computing Omega_beta using the above equation. This is done using the internal function GW_CY5.cc_mobius. First it sorts the curve classes so that for any integer k1, the curve class kbeta is considered after beta. Second, it computes Omega_beta in the given order. This ensures that when Omega_beta is computed, all relevant Omega_betak have already been computed before.\n\nLet us see an example of this. Recall that we have previously computed the Gromov-Witten series of Z=mathcalA_2timesmathbbC^3 in degrees 1 and 2 up to genus 1:\n\njulia> GW[beta]\n(1//12*t2^2*t4*u^2 + 1//12*t2^2*t5*u^2 + 1//12*t2*t4^2*u^2 + 1//12*t2*t4*t5*u^2 + 1//12*t2*t5^2*u^2 - t2)//(t2*t4*t5*u^2 + t4^2*t5*u^2 + t4*t5^2*u^2)\n\njulia> GW[2*beta]\n(1//24*t2^2*t4*u^2 + 1//24*t2^2*t5*u^2 + 1//24*t2*t4^2*u^2 + 1//24*t2*t4*t5*u^2 + 1//24*t2*t5^2*u^2 - 1//8*t2)//(t2*t4*t5*u^2 + t4^2*t5*u^2 + t4*t5^2*u^2)\n\nLet us see how the internal function GW_CY5.cc_mobius turns this into Omega_beta.\n\njulia> Omega_from_cc_mobius = GW_CY5.cc_mobius(GW);\n\njulia> Omega_from_cc_mobius[beta]\n(1//12*t2^2*t4*u^2 + 1//12*t2^2*t5*u^2 + 1//12*t2*t4^2*u^2 + 1//12*t2*t4*t5*u^2 + 1//12*t2*t5^2*u^2 - t2)//(t2*t4*t5*u^2 + t4^2*t5*u^2 + t4*t5^2*u^2)\n\njulia> Omega_from_cc_mobius[2*beta]\n0\n\nIndeed, this is the output of get_Omega_beta:\n\njulia> Omega = get_Omega_beta(G, [beta, 2*beta], max_genus; show_bar=false);\nCalculating b=(1, 0), g=0\nCalculating b=(1, 0), g=1\nCalculating b=(2, 0), g=0\nCalculating b=(2, 0), g=1\n\njulia> Omega[beta]\n(1//12*t2^2*t4*u^2 + 1//12*t2^2*t5*u^2 + 1//12*t2*t4^2*u^2 + 1//12*t2*t4*t5*u^2 + 1//12*t2*t5^2*u^2 - t2)//(t2*t4*t5*u^2 + t4^2*t5*u^2 + t4*t5^2*u^2)\n\njulia> Omega[2*beta]\n0\n\njulia> Omega[beta] == Omega_from_cc_mobius[beta]\ntrue\n\njulia> Omega[2*beta] == Omega_from_cc_mobius[2*beta]\ntrue","title":"Solving the recursion for Omega_beta"},{"category":"section","location":"example/#Testing-conjectures-for-\\Omega_\\beta","page":"Details of the Pipeline","text":"Now that we know how get_Omega_beta computes the true values of Omega_beta (up to some genus) from Gromov-Witten invariants, let us see how it tests the true values vs. the conjectured values when its optional argument check_predictions=true is set.","title":"Testing conjectures for Omega_beta"},{"category":"section","location":"example/#Applying-the-equivariantly-CY-substitution","page":"Details of the Pipeline","text":"Note that the true value Omega[beta] above does not match the predicted value of Omega_beta from above yet, which was as follows:\n\njulia> p = get_Omega_prediction(G, [t1, t2, t3, t4, t5], u, beta, max_genus)\n(-1//12*t3^2*t4*u^2 - 1//12*t3^2*t5*u^2 - 1//12*t3*t4^2*u^2 - 1//4*t3*t4*t5*u^2 - 1//12*t3*t5^2*u^2 - t3 - 1//12*t4^2*t5*u^2 - 1//12*t4*t5^2*u^2 - t4 - t5)//(t3*t4*t5*u^2)\n\njulia> p == Omega[beta]\nfalse\n\nThe reason is that the following attribute is set.\n\njulia> has_attribute(G, :equiCY_substitution)\ntrue\n\nThis means that we still need to impose the equivariantly Calabi-Yau condition on the equivariant parameters of the predicted value. To know what the right substitution is, get_Omega_beta internally does the following.\n\njulia> get_attribute(G, :equiCY_substitution)\n5-element Vector{QQMPolyRingElem}:\n t1\n t2\n -t2 - t4 - t5\n t4\n t5\n\nThis means that, to impose the equivariantly Calabi-Yau condition, we need to substitute epsilon_3 = -epsilon_2-epsilon_4-epsilon_5. Indeed, the difference between the true value Omega[beta] and the predicted value p before imposing the equivariantly Calabi-Yau can be seen to be divisible by epsilon_2+epsilon_3+epsilon_4+epsilon_5:\n\njulia> factor(numerator(p - Omega[beta]))\n(-1//12) * (t2 + t3 + t4 + t5) * (t4 + t5) * (t2*t3*u^2 + t3*t4*u^2 + t3*t5*u^2 + t4*t5*u^2 + 12)\n\nSo let us do the substitution.\n\njulia> t = [t1, t2, t3, t4, t5];\n\njulia> CY_subst = vcat([evaluate(x, t) for x in get_attribute(G, :equiCY_substitution)], [u])\n6-element Vector{QQMPolyRingElem}:\n t1\n t2\n -t2 - t4 - t5\n t4\n t5\n u\n\njulia> prediction_equi_CY = evaluate(p, CY_subst)\n(1//12*t2^2*t4*u^2 + 1//12*t2^2*t5*u^2 + 1//12*t2*t4^2*u^2 + 1//12*t2*t4*t5*u^2 + 1//12*t2*t5^2*u^2 - t2)//(t2*t4*t5*u^2 + t4^2*t5*u^2 + t4*t5^2*u^2)\n\nThis is now the predicted value of Omega_beta after taking into account that we have an equivariantly Calabi-Yau relation between the equivariant parameters. As expected, it matches the true value of Omega_beta computed from Gromov-Witten invariants:\n\njulia> prediction_equi_CY == Omega[beta]\ntrue","title":"Applying the equivariantly CY substitution"},{"category":"section","location":"example/#Conclusion","page":"Details of the Pipeline","text":"Let us see how the entire workflow described above can be executed in a single line of code to test if our conjectured values of Omega_beta and Omega_2beta are correct up to genus 1 in the example of Z=mathcalA_2timesmathbbC^3.\n\njulia> get_Omega_beta(G, [beta, 2*beta], max_genus; check_predictions=true, show_bar=false);\nCalculating b=(1, 0), g=0\nCalculating b=(1, 0), g=1\nCalculating b=(2, 0), g=0\nCalculating b=(2, 0), g=1\nPrediction holds for (2, 0)\nPrediction holds for (1, 0)\nAll predictions hold.\n\nThis shows that the conjectured values of Omega_beta and Omega_2beta are correct up to genus 1.","title":"Conclusion"}]
}
